rso:Requirement_1120835290774482475 a rso:Requirement ;
    rso:belongsToDisclosure rso:Disclosure_Disclosure_306_1_Waste_generation_and_significant ;
    rso:originalText "Background The quantity, type, and quality of waste generated by an organization is a consequence of the activities involved in the production of its products and services (e.g., extraction, processing, procurement of materials, product or service design, production, distribution) and their subsequent consumption. An assessment of how materials move into, through, and out of the organization can help understand where in the organization’s value chain these materials eventually become waste. This provides a holistic overview of waste generation and its causes, which in turn can support the organization in identifying opportunities for waste prevention and for adopting circularity measures. In this way, the organization can go beyond mitigating and remediating negative impacts once waste has been generated and move towards managing waste as a resource."^^xsd:string ;
    rso:pageEnd 8 ;
    rso:pageStart 8 ;
    rso:sectionLabel "RECOMMENDATIONS"^^xsd:string ;
    rso:sourceDocument "GRI 306_ Waste 2020.pdf"^^xsd:string ;
    schema1:identifier "RECOMMENDATIONS"^^xsd:string ;
    schema1:name "RECOMMENDATIONS"^^xsd:string ;
    schema1:text "Background The quantity, type, and quality of waste generated by an organization is a consequence of the activities involved in the production of its products and services (e.g., extraction, processing, procurement of materials, product or service design, production, distribution) and their subsequent consumption. An assessment of how materials move into, through, and out of the organization can help understand where in the organization’s value chain these materials eventually become waste. This provides a holistic overview of waste generation and its causes, which in turn can support the organization in identifying opportunities for waste prevention and for adopting circularity measures. In this way, the organization can go beyond mitigating and remediating negative impacts once waste has been generated and move towards managing waste as a resource."^^xsd:string .

rso:Requirement_1210754655625369426 a rso:Requirement ;
    rso:belongsToDisclosure rso:Disclosure_Disclosure_411_1_Incidents_of_violations_involving ;
    rso:originalText "workers performing the organization’s activities; 2.1.1\\ncommunities likely to be affected by existing or planned activities of the organization. 2.1.2\\nGuidance for Disclosure 411-1 In the context of this disclosure, an ‘incident’ refers to a legal action or complaint registered with the reporting organization or competent authorities through a formal process, or an instance of non-compliance identified by the organization through established procedures. Established procedures to identify instances of non-compliance can include management system audits, formal monitoring programs, or grievance mechanisms."^^xsd:string ;
    rso:pageEnd 8 ;
    rso:pageStart 8 ;
    rso:sectionLabel "RECOMMENDATIONS"^^xsd:string ;
    rso:sourceDocument "GRI 411_ Rights of Indigenous Peoples 2016.pdf"^^xsd:string ;
    schema1:identifier "RECOMMENDATIONS"^^xsd:string ;
    schema1:name "RECOMMENDATIONS"^^xsd:string ;
    schema1:text "workers performing the organization’s activities; 2.1.1\\ncommunities likely to be affected by existing or planned activities of the organization. 2.1.2\\nGuidance for Disclosure 411-1 In the context of this disclosure, an ‘incident’ refers to a legal action or complaint registered with the reporting organization or competent authorities through a formal process, or an instance of non-compliance identified by the organization through established procedures. Established procedures to identify instances of non-compliance can include management system audits, formal monitoring programs, or grievance mechanisms."^^xsd:string .

rso:Requirement_1360487360660006009 a rso:Requirement ;
    rso:belongsToDisclosure rso:Topic_1_Topic_management_disclosures ;
    rso:originalText "1.2\\nWhen reporting on GHG emissions targets, the reporting organization shall explain whether offsets were used to meet the targets, including the type, amount, criteria or scheme of which the offsets are part.\\nThe reporting organization can also: GUIDANCE\\nexplain whether it is subject to any country, regional, or industry-level emissions regulations and policies; and provide examples of these regulations and policies; •\\ndisclose expenditures on treatment of emissions (such as expenditures for filters, agents) and for the purchase and use of emissions certificates. •\\nNote: Requirement 1.2 and Disclosures 305-1 to 305-5 have been superseded by GRI 102: Climate Change 2025\\nGRI 305: Emissions 2016\\n9"^^xsd:string ;
    rso:pageEnd 9 ;
    rso:pageStart 8 ;
    rso:sectionLabel "REQUIREMENTS"^^xsd:string ;
    rso:sourceDocument "GRI 305_ Emissions 2016.pdf"^^xsd:string ;
    schema1:identifier "REQUIREMENTS"^^xsd:string ;
    schema1:name "REQUIREMENTS"^^xsd:string ;
    schema1:text "1.2\\nWhen reporting on GHG emissions targets, the reporting organization shall explain whether offsets were used to meet the targets, including the type, amount, criteria or scheme of which the offsets are part.\\nThe reporting organization can also: GUIDANCE\\nexplain whether it is subject to any country, regional, or industry-level emissions regulations and policies; and provide examples of these regulations and policies; •\\ndisclose expenditures on treatment of emissions (such as expenditures for filters, agents) and for the purchase and use of emissions certificates. •\\nNote: Requirement 1.2 and Disclosures 305-1 to 305-5 have been superseded by GRI 102: Climate Change 2025\\nGRI 305: Emissions 2016\\n9"^^xsd:string .

