rso:Disclosure_Disclosure_101_1_Policies_to_halt_and_reverse_biod a rso:Disclosure ;
    rso:belongsToTopic rso:Topic_GRI_101_Biodiversity_2024_3 ;
    rso:originalText "Disclosure 101-1 Policies to halt and reverse biodiversity loss 7"^^xsd:string,
        "Disclosure 101-1 Policies to halt and reverse biodiversity loss\\nThe organization shall: REQUIREMENTS\\na.\\ndescribe its policies or commitments to halt and reverse biodiversity loss, and how these are informed by the 2050 Goals and 2030 Targets in the Kunming-Montreal Global Biodiversity Framework;\\nreport the extent to which these policies or commitments apply to the organization’s activities and to its business relationships; b.\\nc.\\nreport the goals and targets to halt and reverse biodiversity loss, whether they are informed by scientific consensus, the base year, and the indicators used to evaluate progress.\\nThe Convention on Biological Diversity adopted the Kunming-Montreal Global Biodiversity Framework (hereafter the Global Biodiversity Framework). The Global Biodiversity Framework lays out its 2050 vision of a world ‘living in harmony with nature’ where ‘biodiversity is valued, conserved, restored and wisely used, maintaining ecosystem services, sustaining a healthy planet and delivering benefits essential for all people’."^^xsd:string ;
    rso:pageEnd 3,
        9 ;
    rso:pageStart 3,
        7 ;
    rso:requires rso:Requirement_-1965689406520669386 ;
    rso:sectionLabel "Disclosure 101-1 Policies to halt and reverse biodiversity loss"^^xsd:string,
        "Disclosure 101-1 Policies to halt and reverse biodiversity loss 7"^^xsd:string ;
    rso:sourceDocument "GRI 101_ Biodiversity 2024 - English.pdf"^^xsd:string ;
    schema1:identifier "Disclosure 101-1 Policies to halt and reverse biod"^^xsd:string ;
    schema1:name "Disclosure 101-1 Policies to halt and reverse biodiversity loss"^^xsd:string,
        "Disclosure 101-1 Policies to halt and reverse biodiversity loss 7"^^xsd:string ;
    schema1:text "Disclosure 101-1 Policies to halt and reverse biodiversity loss 7"^^xsd:string,
        "Disclosure 101-1 Policies to halt and reverse biodiversity loss\\nThe organization shall: REQUIREMENTS\\na.\\ndescribe its policies or commitments to halt and reverse biodiversity loss, and how these are informed by the 2050 Goals and 2030 Targets in the Kunming-Montreal Global Biodiversity Framework;\\nreport the extent to which these policies or commitments apply to the organization’s activities and to its business relationships; b.\\nc.\\nreport the goals and targets to halt and reverse biodiversity loss, whether they are informed by scientific consensus, the base year, and the indicators used to evaluate progress.\\nThe Convention on Biological Diversity adopted the Kunming-Montreal Global Biodiversity Framework (hereafter the Global Biodiversity Framework). The Global Biodiversity Framework lays out its 2050 vision of a world ‘living in harmony with nature’ where ‘biodiversity is valued, conserved, restored and wisely used, maintaining ecosystem services, sustaining a healthy planet and delivering benefits essential for all people’."^^xsd:string .

rso:Disclosure_Disclosure_101_2_Management_of_biodiversity_impact a rso:Disclosure ;
    rso:belongsToTopic rso:Topic_GRI_101_Biodiversity_2024_3 ;
    rso:originalText "Disclosure 101-2 Management of biodiversity impacts 9"^^xsd:string,
        "Disclosure 101-2 Management of biodiversity impacts\\nThe organization shall: REQUIREMENTS\\na.\\nreport how it applies the mitigation hierarchy by describing:\\nactions taken to avoid negative impacts on biodiversity; i.\\nactions taken to minimize negative impacts on biodiversity that were not avoided; ii.\\niii.\\nactions taken to restore and rehabilitate affected ecosystems, including the goals of the restoration and rehabilitation, and how stakeholders are engaged throughout the restoration and rehabilitation actions;\\nactions taken to offset residual negative impacts on biodiversity; iv.\\ntransformative actions taken and additional conservation actions taken; v.\\nb.\\nwith reference to 101-2-a-iii, report for each site with the most significant impacts on biodiversity:\\nthe size in hectares of the area under restoration or rehabilitation; i.\\nthe size in hectares of the area restored or rehabilitated; ii.\\nc.\\nwith reference to 101-2-a-iv, report for each offset:\\nthe goals; i.\\nthe geographic location; ii.\\nwhether and how principles of good offset practices are met; iii.\\nwhether and how the offset is certified or verified by a third party; iv.\\nd.\\nlist which of its sites with the most significant impacts on biodiversity have a biodiversity management plan and explain why the other sites do not have a management plan;\\ndescribe how it enhances synergies and reduces trade-offs between actions taken to manage its biodiversity and climate change impacts; e.\\ndescribe how it ensures that the actions taken to manage its impacts on biodiversity avoid and minimize negative impacts and maximize positive impacts for stakeholders. f.\\nThis disclosure provides information on the actions taken by the organization to manage its most significant impacts on biodiversity, including impacts in its supply chain. This disclosure covers the most significant impacts at the sites and for the products and services in the supply chain identified under Disclosure 101-4 . The organization can manage its negative impacts by managing the contribution to the direct drivers of biodiversity loss reported under Disclosure 101-6 (e.g., avoid pollution or minimize greenhouse gas emissions). The organization should also report actions taken to manage impacts downstream in its value chain."^^xsd:string ;
    rso:pageEnd 3,
        14 ;
    rso:pageStart 3,
        9 ;
    rso:requires rso:Requirement_1574516095055135446 ;
    rso:sectionLabel "Disclosure 101-2 Management of biodiversity impacts"^^xsd:string,
        "Disclosure 101-2 Management of biodiversity impacts 9"^^xsd:string ;
    rso:sourceDocument "GRI 101_ Biodiversity 2024 - English.pdf"^^xsd:string ;
    schema1:identifier "Disclosure 101-2 Management of biodiversity impact"^^xsd:string ;
    schema1:name "Disclosure 101-2 Management of biodiversity impacts"^^xsd:string,
        "Disclosure 101-2 Management of biodiversity impacts 9"^^xsd:string ;
    schema1:text "Disclosure 101-2 Management of biodiversity impacts 9"^^xsd:string,
        "Disclosure 101-2 Management of biodiversity impacts\\nThe organization shall: REQUIREMENTS\\na.\\nreport how it applies the mitigation hierarchy by describing:\\nactions taken to avoid negative impacts on biodiversity; i.\\nactions taken to minimize negative impacts on biodiversity that were not avoided; ii.\\niii.\\nactions taken to restore and rehabilitate affected ecosystems, including the goals of the restoration and rehabilitation, and how stakeholders are engaged throughout the restoration and rehabilitation actions;\\nactions taken to offset residual negative impacts on biodiversity; iv.\\ntransformative actions taken and additional conservation actions taken; v.\\nb.\\nwith reference to 101-2-a-iii, report for each site with the most significant impacts on biodiversity:\\nthe size in hectares of the area under restoration or rehabilitation; i.\\nthe size in hectares of the area restored or rehabilitated; ii.\\nc.\\nwith reference to 101-2-a-iv, report for each offset:\\nthe goals; i.\\nthe geographic location; ii.\\nwhether and how principles of good offset practices are met; iii.\\nwhether and how the offset is certified or verified by a third party; iv.\\nd.\\nlist which of its sites with the most significant impacts on biodiversity have a biodiversity management plan and explain why the other sites do not have a management plan;\\ndescribe how it enhances synergies and reduces trade-offs between actions taken to manage its biodiversity and climate change impacts; e.\\ndescribe how it ensures that the actions taken to manage its impacts on biodiversity avoid and minimize negative impacts and maximize positive impacts for stakeholders. f.\\nThis disclosure provides information on the actions taken by the organization to manage its most significant impacts on biodiversity, including impacts in its supply chain. This disclosure covers the most significant impacts at the sites and for the products and services in the supply chain identified under Disclosure 101-4 . The organization can manage its negative impacts by managing the contribution to the direct drivers of biodiversity loss reported under Disclosure 101-6 (e.g., avoid pollution or minimize greenhouse gas emissions). The organization should also report actions taken to manage impacts downstream in its value chain."^^xsd:string .

rso:Disclosure_Disclosure_101_3_Access_and_benefit_sharing a rso:Disclosure ;
    rso:belongsToTopic rso:Topic_GRI_101_Biodiversity_2024_3 ;
    rso:originalText "Disclosure 101-3 Access and benefit-sharing\\nThe organization shall: REQUIREMENTS\\ndescribe the process to ensure compliance with access and benefit-sharing regulations and measures; a.\\ndescribe voluntary actions taken to advance access and benefit-sharing that are additional to legal obligations or when there are no regulations and measures. b.\\nThis disclosure provides information on how the organization complies with access and benefit- sharing (ABS) regulations and measures regarding access to genetic resources and associated traditional knowledge held by Indigenous Peoples and local communities. These regulations and measures also establish the rules on fair and equitable benefit-sharing arising from the utilization of genetic resources and the associated traditional knowledge. It also provides information on the voluntary actions taken by the organization to advance access and fair and equitable benefit-sharing."^^xsd:string ;
    rso:pageEnd 16 ;
    rso:pageStart 14 ;
    rso:requires rso:Requirement_6137751956780085554 ;
    rso:sectionLabel "Disclosure 101-3 Access and benefit-sharing"^^xsd:string ;
    rso:sourceDocument "GRI 101_ Biodiversity 2024 - English.pdf"^^xsd:string ;
    schema1:identifier "Disclosure 101-3 Access and benefit-sharing"^^xsd:string ;
    schema1:name "Disclosure 101-3 Access and benefit-sharing"^^xsd:string ;
    schema1:text "Disclosure 101-3 Access and benefit-sharing\\nThe organization shall: REQUIREMENTS\\ndescribe the process to ensure compliance with access and benefit-sharing regulations and measures; a.\\ndescribe voluntary actions taken to advance access and benefit-sharing that are additional to legal obligations or when there are no regulations and measures. b.\\nThis disclosure provides information on how the organization complies with access and benefit- sharing (ABS) regulations and measures regarding access to genetic resources and associated traditional knowledge held by Indigenous Peoples and local communities. These regulations and measures also establish the rules on fair and equitable benefit-sharing arising from the utilization of genetic resources and the associated traditional knowledge. It also provides information on the voluntary actions taken by the organization to advance access and fair and equitable benefit-sharing."^^xsd:string .

rso:Disclosure_Disclosure_101_4_Identification_of_biodiversity_im a rso:Disclosure ;
    rso:belongsToTopic rso:Topic_GRI_101_Biodiversity_2024_3 ;
    rso:originalText "Disclosure 101-4 Identification of biodiversity impacts 16"^^xsd:string,
        "Disclosure 101-4 Identification of biodiversity impacts\\nThe organization shall: REQUIREMENTS\\nexplain how it has determined which of its sites and which products and services in its supply chain have the most significant actual and potential impacts on biodiversity. a.\\nThis disclosure enables the organization to explain how it has determined which of its sites and which products and services in its supply chain have the most significant actual and potential impacts on biodiversity. It covers products and services from suppliers throughout the organization’s supply chain, including from suppliers beyond the first tier. This provides an understanding of where in the supply chain, potentially many tiers removed from the organization, the most significant impacts on biodiversity are. The organization can additionally report the information for entities downstream in its value chain."^^xsd:string ;
    rso:pageEnd 3,
        20 ;
    rso:pageStart 3,
        16 ;
    rso:requires rso:Requirement_8586334176835271782 ;
    rso:sectionLabel "Disclosure 101-4 Identification of biodiversity impacts"^^xsd:string,
        "Disclosure 101-4 Identification of biodiversity impacts 16"^^xsd:string ;
    rso:sourceDocument "GRI 101_ Biodiversity 2024 - English.pdf"^^xsd:string ;
    schema1:identifier "Disclosure 101-4 Identification of biodiversity im"^^xsd:string ;
    schema1:name "Disclosure 101-4 Identification of biodiversity impacts"^^xsd:string,
        "Disclosure 101-4 Identification of biodiversity impacts 16"^^xsd:string ;
    schema1:text "Disclosure 101-4 Identification of biodiversity impacts 16"^^xsd:string,
        "Disclosure 101-4 Identification of biodiversity impacts\\nThe organization shall: REQUIREMENTS\\nexplain how it has determined which of its sites and which products and services in its supply chain have the most significant actual and potential impacts on biodiversity. a.\\nThis disclosure enables the organization to explain how it has determined which of its sites and which products and services in its supply chain have the most significant actual and potential impacts on biodiversity. It covers products and services from suppliers throughout the organization’s supply chain, including from suppliers beyond the first tier. This provides an understanding of where in the supply chain, potentially many tiers removed from the organization, the most significant impacts on biodiversity are. The organization can additionally report the information for entities downstream in its value chain."^^xsd:string .

rso:Disclosure_Disclosure_101_5_Locations_with_biodiversity_impac a rso:Disclosure ;
    rso:belongsToTopic rso:Topic_GRI_101_Biodiversity_2024_3 ;
    rso:originalText "Disclosure 101-5 Locations with biodiversity impacts 20"^^xsd:string,
        "Disclosure 101-5 Locations with biodiversity impacts\\nThe organization shall: REQUIREMENTS\\nreport the location and size in hectares of its sites with the most significant impacts on biodiversity; a.\\nb.\\nfor each site reported under 101-5-a, report whether it is in or near an ecologically sensitive area, the distance to these areas, and whether these are:\\nareas of biodiversity importance; i.\\nareas of high ecosystem integrity; ii.\\nareas of rapid decline in ecosystem integrity; iii.\\nareas of high physical water risks; iv.\\nareas important for the delivery of ecosystem service benefits to Indigenous Peoples, local communities, and other stakeholders; v.\\nreport the activities that take place in each site reported under 101-5-a; c.\\nd.\\nreport the products and services in its supply chain with the most significant impacts on biodiversity and the countries or jurisdictions where the activities associated with these products and services take place.\\nThis disclosure provides information about the locations of the organization’s sites with the most significant impacts on biodiversity. It also provides information on the location of the activities associated with the products and services in its supply chain with the most significant impacts on biodiversity. The sites and products and services with the most significant impacts on biodiversity are identified under Disclosure 101-4 . These sites and products and services are the focus of Disclosure 101-2 and Disclosures 101-5 to 101-8 of this Standard."^^xsd:string ;
    rso:pageEnd 3,
        23 ;
    rso:pageStart 3,
        20 ;
    rso:requires rso:Requirement_3964760057285804431 ;
    rso:sectionLabel "Disclosure 101-5 Locations with biodiversity impacts"^^xsd:string,
        "Disclosure 101-5 Locations with biodiversity impacts 20"^^xsd:string ;
    rso:sourceDocument "GRI 101_ Biodiversity 2024 - English.pdf"^^xsd:string ;
    schema1:identifier "Disclosure 101-5 Locations with biodiversity impac"^^xsd:string ;
    schema1:name "Disclosure 101-5 Locations with biodiversity impacts"^^xsd:string,
        "Disclosure 101-5 Locations with biodiversity impacts 20"^^xsd:string ;
    schema1:text "Disclosure 101-5 Locations with biodiversity impacts 20"^^xsd:string,
        "Disclosure 101-5 Locations with biodiversity impacts\\nThe organization shall: REQUIREMENTS\\nreport the location and size in hectares of its sites with the most significant impacts on biodiversity; a.\\nb.\\nfor each site reported under 101-5-a, report whether it is in or near an ecologically sensitive area, the distance to these areas, and whether these are:\\nareas of biodiversity importance; i.\\nareas of high ecosystem integrity; ii.\\nareas of rapid decline in ecosystem integrity; iii.\\nareas of high physical water risks; iv.\\nareas important for the delivery of ecosystem service benefits to Indigenous Peoples, local communities, and other stakeholders; v.\\nreport the activities that take place in each site reported under 101-5-a; c.\\nd.\\nreport the products and services in its supply chain with the most significant impacts on biodiversity and the countries or jurisdictions where the activities associated with these products and services take place.\\nThis disclosure provides information about the locations of the organization’s sites with the most significant impacts on biodiversity. It also provides information on the location of the activities associated with the products and services in its supply chain with the most significant impacts on biodiversity. The sites and products and services with the most significant impacts on biodiversity are identified under Disclosure 101-4 . These sites and products and services are the focus of Disclosure 101-2 and Disclosures 101-5 to 101-8 of this Standard."^^xsd:string .

rso:Disclosure_Disclosure_101_6_Direct_drivers_of_biodiversity_lo a rso:Disclosure ;
    rso:belongsToTopic rso:Topic_GRI_101_Biodiversity_2024_3 ;
    rso:originalText "Disclosure 101-6 Direct drivers of biodiversity loss 23"^^xsd:string,
        "Disclosure 101-6 Direct drivers of biodiversity loss\\nThe organization shall: REQUIREMENTS\\na.\\nfor each site reported under 101-5-a where its activities lead or could lead to land and sea use change, report:\\ni.\\nthe size in hectares of natural ecosystem converted since a cut-off or reference date, the cut-off date or reference date, and the type of ecosystem before and after conversion;\\nii.\\nthe size in hectares of land and sea converted from one intensively used or modified ecosystem to another during the reporting period, and the type of ecosystem before and after conversion;\\nb.\\nfor each site reported under 101-5-a where its activities lead or could lead to the exploitation of natural resources, report:\\nfor each wild species harvested, the quantity, the type, and extinction risk; i.\\nwater withdrawal and water consumption in megaliters; ii.\\nfor each site reported under 101-5-a where its activities lead or could lead to pollution, report the quantity and the type of each pollutant generated; c.\\nd.\\nfor each site reported under 101-5-a where its activities lead or could lead to the introduction of invasive alien species, describe how invasive alien species are or may be introduced;\\ne.\\nfor each product and service in its supply chain reported under 101-5-d, report the information required under 101-6-a, 101-6-b, 101-6-c, and 101-6-d, with a breakdown by country or jurisdiction;\\nreport contextual information necessary to understand how the data has been compiled, including standards, methodologies, and assumptions used. f.\\nThis disclosure provides an understanding of the direct drivers of biodiversity loss (hereafter the direct drivers) leading to the most significant impacts. The organization should additionally report the information on the direct drivers for its downstream value chain."^^xsd:string ;
    rso:pageEnd 3,
        26 ;
    rso:pageStart 3,
        23 ;
    rso:requires rso:Requirement_-8862423717014516624 ;
    rso:sectionLabel "Disclosure 101-6 Direct drivers of biodiversity loss"^^xsd:string,
        "Disclosure 101-6 Direct drivers of biodiversity loss 23"^^xsd:string ;
    rso:sourceDocument "GRI 101_ Biodiversity 2024 - English.pdf"^^xsd:string ;
    schema1:identifier "Disclosure 101-6 Direct drivers of biodiversity lo"^^xsd:string ;
    schema1:name "Disclosure 101-6 Direct drivers of biodiversity loss"^^xsd:string,
        "Disclosure 101-6 Direct drivers of biodiversity loss 23"^^xsd:string ;
    schema1:text "Disclosure 101-6 Direct drivers of biodiversity loss 23"^^xsd:string,
        "Disclosure 101-6 Direct drivers of biodiversity loss\\nThe organization shall: REQUIREMENTS\\na.\\nfor each site reported under 101-5-a where its activities lead or could lead to land and sea use change, report:\\ni.\\nthe size in hectares of natural ecosystem converted since a cut-off or reference date, the cut-off date or reference date, and the type of ecosystem before and after conversion;\\nii.\\nthe size in hectares of land and sea converted from one intensively used or modified ecosystem to another during the reporting period, and the type of ecosystem before and after conversion;\\nb.\\nfor each site reported under 101-5-a where its activities lead or could lead to the exploitation of natural resources, report:\\nfor each wild species harvested, the quantity, the type, and extinction risk; i.\\nwater withdrawal and water consumption in megaliters; ii.\\nfor each site reported under 101-5-a where its activities lead or could lead to pollution, report the quantity and the type of each pollutant generated; c.\\nd.\\nfor each site reported under 101-5-a where its activities lead or could lead to the introduction of invasive alien species, describe how invasive alien species are or may be introduced;\\ne.\\nfor each product and service in its supply chain reported under 101-5-d, report the information required under 101-6-a, 101-6-b, 101-6-c, and 101-6-d, with a breakdown by country or jurisdiction;\\nreport contextual information necessary to understand how the data has been compiled, including standards, methodologies, and assumptions used. f.\\nThis disclosure provides an understanding of the direct drivers of biodiversity loss (hereafter the direct drivers) leading to the most significant impacts. The organization should additionally report the information on the direct drivers for its downstream value chain."^^xsd:string .

rso:Disclosure_Disclosure_101_7_Changes_to_the_state_of_biodivers a rso:Disclosure ;
    rso:belongsToTopic rso:Topic_GRI_101_Biodiversity_2024_3 ;
    rso:originalText "Disclosure 101-7 Changes to the state of biodiversity 28"^^xsd:string,
        "Disclosure 101-7 Changes to the state of biodiversity\\nThe organization shall: REQUIREMENTS\\na.\\nfor each site reported under 101-5-a, report the following information on affected or potentially affected ecosystems:\\nthe ecosystem type for the base year; i.\\nthe ecosystem size in hectares for the base year; ii.\\nthe ecosystem condition for the base year and the current reporting period; iii.\\nreport contextual information necessary to understand how the data has been compiled, including standards, methodologies, and assumptions used. b.\\nThis disclosure provides information about the changes in the condition of the ecosystem affected or potentially affected by the organization."^^xsd:string ;
    rso:pageEnd 3,
        31 ;
    rso:pageStart 3,
        28 ;
    rso:requires rso:Requirement_4550583138918485504 ;
    rso:sectionLabel "Disclosure 101-7 Changes to the state of biodiversity"^^xsd:string,
        "Disclosure 101-7 Changes to the state of biodiversity 28"^^xsd:string ;
    rso:sourceDocument "GRI 101_ Biodiversity 2024 - English.pdf"^^xsd:string ;
    schema1:identifier "Disclosure 101-7 Changes to the state of biodivers"^^xsd:string ;
    schema1:name "Disclosure 101-7 Changes to the state of biodiversity"^^xsd:string,
        "Disclosure 101-7 Changes to the state of biodiversity 28"^^xsd:string ;
    schema1:text "Disclosure 101-7 Changes to the state of biodiversity 28"^^xsd:string,
        "Disclosure 101-7 Changes to the state of biodiversity\\nThe organization shall: REQUIREMENTS\\na.\\nfor each site reported under 101-5-a, report the following information on affected or potentially affected ecosystems:\\nthe ecosystem type for the base year; i.\\nthe ecosystem size in hectares for the base year; ii.\\nthe ecosystem condition for the base year and the current reporting period; iii.\\nreport contextual information necessary to understand how the data has been compiled, including standards, methodologies, and assumptions used. b.\\nThis disclosure provides information about the changes in the condition of the ecosystem affected or potentially affected by the organization."^^xsd:string .

rso:Disclosure_Disclosure_101_8_Ecosystem_services a rso:Disclosure ;
    rso:belongsToTopic rso:Topic_GRI_101_Biodiversity_2024_3 ;
    rso:originalText "Disclosure 101-8 Ecosystem services\\nThe organization shall: REQUIREMENTS\\nfor each site reported under 101-5-a, list the ecosystem services and beneficiaries affected or potentially affected by the organization’s activities; a.\\nexplain how the ecosystem services and beneficiaries are or could be affected by the organization's activities. b.\\nEcosystem services occur through an ecosystem's normal functioning and can fall into one or more of the following categories:"^^xsd:string ;
    rso:pageEnd 49 ;
    rso:pageStart 31 ;
    rso:requires rso:Requirement_-579668400706264259 ;
    rso:sectionLabel "Disclosure 101-8 Ecosystem services"^^xsd:string ;
    rso:sourceDocument "GRI 101_ Biodiversity 2024 - English.pdf"^^xsd:string ;
    schema1:identifier "Disclosure 101-8 Ecosystem services"^^xsd:string ;
    schema1:name "Disclosure 101-8 Ecosystem services"^^xsd:string ;
    schema1:text "Disclosure 101-8 Ecosystem services\\nThe organization shall: REQUIREMENTS\\nfor each site reported under 101-5-a, list the ecosystem services and beneficiaries affected or potentially affected by the organization’s activities; a.\\nexplain how the ecosystem services and beneficiaries are or could be affected by the organization's activities. b.\\nEcosystem services occur through an ecosystem's normal functioning and can fall into one or more of the following categories:"^^xsd:string .

rso:Disclosure_Disclosure_102_10_Carbon_credits a rso:Disclosure ;
    rso:belongsToTopic rso:Topic_GRI_102_Climate_Change_2025 ;
    rso:originalText "Disclosure 102-10 Carbon credits\\nThe organization shall: REQUIREMENTS\\nreport the total amount of carbon credits canceled in metric tons of CO ₂ equivalent and a breakdown of this total by removal or reduction projects; a.\\nb.\\nfor each project where carbon credits have been canceled, report:\\nproject name and ID; i.\\nproject type; ii.\\ncancelation serial number, cancellation date, and vintage; iii.\\nhost country and issuing registry; iv.\\nc.\\nfor each carbon credit project reported under 102-10-b, describe how the project adheres to each of the following quality criteria:\\nadditionality; i.\\ncredible baselines; ii.\\npermanence; iii.\\nleakage avoidance; iv.\\nunique issuance and claiming; v.\\nregular monitoring; vi.\\nindependent validation and verification; vii.\\nGHG program governance; viii.\\nreport the purpose of carbon credit cancelation; d.\\ne.\\ndescribe the impacts on people and the environment from projects where carbon credits are purchased and how the organization continuously monitors and evaluates them, including:\\nthe categories of stakeholders consulted in project implementation; i.\\nhow human rights are respected; ii.\\nhow socio-economic benefits are provided to local communities and Indigenous Peoples; iii.\\nhow biodiversity is conserved; iv.\\nhow trade-offs are assessed. v.\\nThis disclosure aims to increase transparency regarding the use of carbon credits."^^xsd:string ;
    rso:pageEnd 41 ;
    rso:pageStart 38 ;
    rso:requires rso:Requirement_5020729406828508695 ;
    rso:sectionLabel "Disclosure 102-10 Carbon credits"^^xsd:string ;
    rso:sourceDocument "GRI 102_ Climate Change 2025.pdf"^^xsd:string ;
    schema1:identifier "Disclosure 102-10 Carbon credits"^^xsd:string ;
    schema1:name "Disclosure 102-10 Carbon credits"^^xsd:string ;
    schema1:text "Disclosure 102-10 Carbon credits\\nThe organization shall: REQUIREMENTS\\nreport the total amount of carbon credits canceled in metric tons of CO ₂ equivalent and a breakdown of this total by removal or reduction projects; a.\\nb.\\nfor each project where carbon credits have been canceled, report:\\nproject name and ID; i.\\nproject type; ii.\\ncancelation serial number, cancellation date, and vintage; iii.\\nhost country and issuing registry; iv.\\nc.\\nfor each carbon credit project reported under 102-10-b, describe how the project adheres to each of the following quality criteria:\\nadditionality; i.\\ncredible baselines; ii.\\npermanence; iii.\\nleakage avoidance; iv.\\nunique issuance and claiming; v.\\nregular monitoring; vi.\\nindependent validation and verification; vii.\\nGHG program governance; viii.\\nreport the purpose of carbon credit cancelation; d.\\ne.\\ndescribe the impacts on people and the environment from projects where carbon credits are purchased and how the organization continuously monitors and evaluates them, including:\\nthe categories of stakeholders consulted in project implementation; i.\\nhow human rights are respected; ii.\\nhow socio-economic benefits are provided to local communities and Indigenous Peoples; iii.\\nhow biodiversity is conserved; iv.\\nhow trade-offs are assessed. v.\\nThis disclosure aims to increase transparency regarding the use of carbon credits."^^xsd:string .

rso:Disclosure_Disclosure_102_1_Transition_plan_for_climate_chang a rso:Disclosure ;
    rso:belongsToTopic rso:Topic_GRI_102_Climate_Change_2025 ;
    rso:originalText "Disclosure 102-1 Transition plan for climate change mitigation 7"^^xsd:string,
        "Disclosure 102-1 Transition plan for climate change mitigation\\nThe organization shall: REQUIREMENTS\\ndescribe its transition plan, including policies and actions to mitigate climate change; a.\\nb.\\ndescribe how the transition plan aligns with the latest scientific evidence on the global effort needed to limit global warming to 1.5 o C, including the source of the climate change-related scenarios used, and the methodologies and assumptions used to develop the transition plan;\\nreport the total expenditure incurred by the implementation of the transition plan as monetary value and percentage of the total expenditure incurred in the reporting period; c.\\nreport the governance bodies or individual roles responsible for overseeing and implementing the transition plan and their responsibilities; d.\\ndescribe how the transition plan is embedded in its business strategy; e.\\nf.\\nreport the targets to achieve the transition plan and progress toward them, including:\\nGHG emissions reduction targets reported under Disclosure 102-4 ; i.\\ntargets to phase out fossil fuels, the base year, and standards, methodologies, and assumptions used to set the targets; ii.\\nother climate change mitigation targets, how these were set, what is covered, the base year, and their role within the transition plan; iii.\\ndescribe how the transition plan aligns with just transition principles and how engagement with stakeholders informs its development and implementation; g.\\nh.\\ndescribe the impacts on people and the environment from implementing the transition plan and the actions taken to manage them, including:\\nworkers, local communities, and Indigenous Peoples; i.\\nbiodiversity; ii.\\ndescribe how its public policy activities, including lobbying activities, are consistent with the transition plan; i.\\nexplain, in the absence of a transition plan, why it does not exist, and describe the steps being taken to develop it and the expected time frame. j.\\nThis disclosure provides information about the organization’s transition plan to mitigate climate change. It covers the organization’s activities and its upstream and downstream value chain."^^xsd:string ;
    rso:pageEnd 3,
        12 ;
    rso:pageStart 3,
        7 ;
    rso:requires rso:Requirement_-5156102795970853435 ;
    rso:sectionLabel "Disclosure 102-1 Transition plan for climate change mitigation"^^xsd:string,
        "Disclosure 102-1 Transition plan for climate change mitigation 7"^^xsd:string ;
    rso:sourceDocument "GRI 102_ Climate Change 2025.pdf"^^xsd:string ;
    schema1:identifier "Disclosure 102-1 Transition plan for climate chang"^^xsd:string ;
    schema1:name "Disclosure 102-1 Transition plan for climate change mitigation"^^xsd:string,
        "Disclosure 102-1 Transition plan for climate change mitigation 7"^^xsd:string ;
    schema1:text "Disclosure 102-1 Transition plan for climate change mitigation 7"^^xsd:string,
        "Disclosure 102-1 Transition plan for climate change mitigation\\nThe organization shall: REQUIREMENTS\\ndescribe its transition plan, including policies and actions to mitigate climate change; a.\\nb.\\ndescribe how the transition plan aligns with the latest scientific evidence on the global effort needed to limit global warming to 1.5 o C, including the source of the climate change-related scenarios used, and the methodologies and assumptions used to develop the transition plan;\\nreport the total expenditure incurred by the implementation of the transition plan as monetary value and percentage of the total expenditure incurred in the reporting period; c.\\nreport the governance bodies or individual roles responsible for overseeing and implementing the transition plan and their responsibilities; d.\\ndescribe how the transition plan is embedded in its business strategy; e.\\nf.\\nreport the targets to achieve the transition plan and progress toward them, including:\\nGHG emissions reduction targets reported under Disclosure 102-4 ; i.\\ntargets to phase out fossil fuels, the base year, and standards, methodologies, and assumptions used to set the targets; ii.\\nother climate change mitigation targets, how these were set, what is covered, the base year, and their role within the transition plan; iii.\\ndescribe how the transition plan aligns with just transition principles and how engagement with stakeholders informs its development and implementation; g.\\nh.\\ndescribe the impacts on people and the environment from implementing the transition plan and the actions taken to manage them, including:\\nworkers, local communities, and Indigenous Peoples; i.\\nbiodiversity; ii.\\ndescribe how its public policy activities, including lobbying activities, are consistent with the transition plan; i.\\nexplain, in the absence of a transition plan, why it does not exist, and describe the steps being taken to develop it and the expected time frame. j.\\nThis disclosure provides information about the organization’s transition plan to mitigate climate change. It covers the organization’s activities and its upstream and downstream value chain."^^xsd:string .

rso:Disclosure_Disclosure_102_2_Climate_change_adaptation_plan a rso:Disclosure ;
    rso:belongsToTopic rso:Topic_GRI_102_Climate_Change_2025 ;
    rso:originalText "Disclosure 102-2 Climate change adaptation plan\\nThe organization shall: REQUIREMENTS\\na.\\ndescribe the impacts on people and the environment associated with its climate change-related risks and opportunities and how they were considered in the development of the adaptation plan;\\nb.\\ndescribe its adaptation plan, including:\\npolicies and actions to adapt to climate change; i.\\nii.\\nthe source of the climate change-related scenarios used, the temperature projection included in the scenarios, and the methodologies and assumptions used to develop the adaptation plan;\\niii.\\nthe total expenditure incurred by the implementation of the adaptation plan as monetary value and percentage of the total expenditure incurred in the reporting period;\\nthe governance bodies or individual roles responsible for overseeing and implementing the adaptation plan and their responsibilities; iv.\\nthe targets to achieve the adaptation plan and progress toward them; v.\\nhow the adaptation plan aligns with just transition principles and how engagement with stakeholders informs its development and implementation; vi.\\nc.\\ndescribe the impacts on people and the environment from implementing the adaptation plan and the actions taken to manage them, including for:\\nworkers, local communities, and Indigenous Peoples; i.\\nbiodiversity; ii.\\nexplain, in the absence of an adaptation plan, why it does not exist, and describe the steps being taken to develop it and the expected time frame. d.\\nThis disclosure provides information about the organization’s plan to adapt to the effects of climate change. It covers the organization’s activities and its upstream and downstream value chain."^^xsd:string ;
    rso:pageEnd 16 ;
    rso:pageStart 13 ;
    rso:requires rso:Requirement_5879767961458577923 ;
    rso:sectionLabel "Disclosure 102-2 Climate change adaptation plan"^^xsd:string ;
    rso:sourceDocument "GRI 102_ Climate Change 2025.pdf"^^xsd:string ;
    schema1:identifier "Disclosure 102-2 Climate change adaptation plan"^^xsd:string ;
    schema1:name "Disclosure 102-2 Climate change adaptation plan"^^xsd:string ;
    schema1:text "Disclosure 102-2 Climate change adaptation plan\\nThe organization shall: REQUIREMENTS\\na.\\ndescribe the impacts on people and the environment associated with its climate change-related risks and opportunities and how they were considered in the development of the adaptation plan;\\nb.\\ndescribe its adaptation plan, including:\\npolicies and actions to adapt to climate change; i.\\nii.\\nthe source of the climate change-related scenarios used, the temperature projection included in the scenarios, and the methodologies and assumptions used to develop the adaptation plan;\\niii.\\nthe total expenditure incurred by the implementation of the adaptation plan as monetary value and percentage of the total expenditure incurred in the reporting period;\\nthe governance bodies or individual roles responsible for overseeing and implementing the adaptation plan and their responsibilities; iv.\\nthe targets to achieve the adaptation plan and progress toward them; v.\\nhow the adaptation plan aligns with just transition principles and how engagement with stakeholders informs its development and implementation; vi.\\nc.\\ndescribe the impacts on people and the environment from implementing the adaptation plan and the actions taken to manage them, including for:\\nworkers, local communities, and Indigenous Peoples; i.\\nbiodiversity; ii.\\nexplain, in the absence of an adaptation plan, why it does not exist, and describe the steps being taken to develop it and the expected time frame. d.\\nThis disclosure provides information about the organization’s plan to adapt to the effects of climate change. It covers the organization’s activities and its upstream and downstream value chain."^^xsd:string .

rso:Disclosure_Disclosure_102_3_Just_transition a rso:Disclosure ;
    rso:belongsToTopic rso:Topic_GRI_102_Climate_Change_2025 ;
    rso:originalText "Disclosure 102-3 Just transition\\nIn the context of its transition or adaptation efforts, the organization shall: REQUIREMENTS\\na.\\nreport the total number of new employees recruited and a breakdown of this total by:\\ngender; i.\\nemployee type; ii.\\nb.\\nreport the total number of employees whose work was terminated and a breakdown of this total by:\\ngender; i.\\nemployee type; ii.\\nc.\\nreport the total number of redeployed employees and a breakdown of this total by:\\ngender; i.\\nemployee type; ii.\\nd.\\nreport the total number of employees who received training for up- and re-skilling, and a breakdown of this total by:\\ngender; i.\\nemployee type; ii.\\nreport the total number of new workers who are not employees recruited and a breakdown of this total by gender; e.\\nreport the total number of workers who are not employees whose work was terminated and a breakdown of this total by gender; f.\\ng.\\nreport the total number and percentage of new employees recruited whose basic pay is at or above the cost-of-living estimate, and describe actions taken or commitments made to address any gaps between basic pay and the cost-of-living estimate for workers reported under 102-3-a and 102-3-e;\\nlist the locations of operation where the organization has impacts on local communities and Indigenous Peoples; h.\\ni.\\nreport the percentage of locations of operation listed under 102-3-h in which an agreement has been reached with affected or potentially affected local communities or Indigenous Peoples to safeguard their interests;\\nj.\\nreport contextual information necessary to understand the data reported under 102-3 and describe the methodologies and assumptions used to compile the data, including whether the numbers are reported:\\nin head count, full-time equivalent (FTE), or using another methodology; i.\\nat the end of the reporting period, as an average across the reporting period, or using another methodology. ii.\\nThis disclosure describes the impacts of the organization's transition or adaptation efforts on workers, local communities, and Indigenous Peoples. Managing these impacts leads to a just transition."^^xsd:string ;
    rso:pageEnd 21 ;
    rso:pageStart 18 ;
    rso:requires rso:Requirement_6242004780163063369 ;
    rso:sectionLabel "Disclosure 102-3 Just transition"^^xsd:string ;
    rso:sourceDocument "GRI 102_ Climate Change 2025.pdf"^^xsd:string ;
    schema1:identifier "Disclosure 102-3 Just transition"^^xsd:string ;
    schema1:name "Disclosure 102-3 Just transition"^^xsd:string ;
    schema1:text "Disclosure 102-3 Just transition\\nIn the context of its transition or adaptation efforts, the organization shall: REQUIREMENTS\\na.\\nreport the total number of new employees recruited and a breakdown of this total by:\\ngender; i.\\nemployee type; ii.\\nb.\\nreport the total number of employees whose work was terminated and a breakdown of this total by:\\ngender; i.\\nemployee type; ii.\\nc.\\nreport the total number of redeployed employees and a breakdown of this total by:\\ngender; i.\\nemployee type; ii.\\nd.\\nreport the total number of employees who received training for up- and re-skilling, and a breakdown of this total by:\\ngender; i.\\nemployee type; ii.\\nreport the total number of new workers who are not employees recruited and a breakdown of this total by gender; e.\\nreport the total number of workers who are not employees whose work was terminated and a breakdown of this total by gender; f.\\ng.\\nreport the total number and percentage of new employees recruited whose basic pay is at or above the cost-of-living estimate, and describe actions taken or commitments made to address any gaps between basic pay and the cost-of-living estimate for workers reported under 102-3-a and 102-3-e;\\nlist the locations of operation where the organization has impacts on local communities and Indigenous Peoples; h.\\ni.\\nreport the percentage of locations of operation listed under 102-3-h in which an agreement has been reached with affected or potentially affected local communities or Indigenous Peoples to safeguard their interests;\\nj.\\nreport contextual information necessary to understand the data reported under 102-3 and describe the methodologies and assumptions used to compile the data, including whether the numbers are reported:\\nin head count, full-time equivalent (FTE), or using another methodology; i.\\nat the end of the reporting period, as an average across the reporting period, or using another methodology. ii.\\nThis disclosure describes the impacts of the organization's transition or adaptation efforts on workers, local communities, and Indigenous Peoples. Managing these impacts leads to a just transition."^^xsd:string .

rso:Disclosure_Disclosure_102_4_GHG_emissions_reduction_targets_a a rso:Disclosure ;
    rso:belongsToTopic rso:Topic_GRI_102_Climate_Change_2025 ;
    rso:originalText "Disclosure 102-4 GHG emissions reduction targets and progress 21"^^xsd:string,
        "Disclosure 102-4 GHG emissions reduction targets and progress\\nThe organization shall: REQUIREMENTS\\na.\\nreport short-, medium-, and long-term gross Scope 1, Scope 2, and Scope 3 GHG emissions reduction targets in metric tons of CO ₂ equivalent and as a percentage of base year emissions, where:\\ngross Scope 1, Scope 2, and Scope 3 GHG emissions reduction targets are reported separately or where Scope 1 and Scope 2 GHG emissions are combined; i.\\ngross Scope 1 and Scope 2 GHG emissions reduction targets cover the total Scope 1 and Scope 2 GHG emissions reported under Disclosures 102-5 and 102-6; ii.\\nGHG removals, GHG trades, and avoided GHG emissions are excluded; iii.\\nfor each gross GHG emissions reduction target, report whether biogenic CO ₂ emissions are included in the target; b.\\nfor each gross Scope 2 GHG emissions reduction target, report whether the targets use the location-based or market-based method; c.\\nfor each gross Scope 3 GHG emissions reduction target, list the Scope 3 categories covered by the targets; d.\\nfor each gross GHG emissions reduction target, report the gases covered by the target; e.\\nexplain how the gross GHG emissions reduction targets align with the latest scientific evidence on the effort needed to limit global warming to 1.5°C; f.\\ndescribe its gross GHG emissions reduction target revision policy; g.\\nh.\\nfor each gross GHG emissions reduction target, report the base year, including:\\nthe rationale for choosing it; i.\\nbase year emissions in metric tons of CO 2 equivalent; ii.\\nthe context for any significant changes in emissions that triggered recalculations of base year emissions; iii.\\nthe previously reported base year emissions, if base year emissions are recalculated; iv.\\ni.\\nreport the progress toward each gross GHG emissions reduction target using the inventory method, in metric tons of CO 2 equivalent, and as a percentage of a base year emissions;\\nj.\\nfor each gross GHG emissions reduction target, explain how the progress toward the target was achieved and whether it is due to:\\nreductions as a result of the organization’s initiatives; or i.\\nother factors; ii.\\nreport standards, methodologies, assumptions, and calculation tools used. k.\\nThe GHG emissions reduction targets reported under this disclosure are used to report the targets to achieve the transition plan under 102-1-f ."^^xsd:string ;
    rso:pageEnd 3,
        25 ;
    rso:pageStart 3,
        21 ;
    rso:requires rso:Requirement_-6898199046292519936 ;
    rso:sectionLabel "Disclosure 102-4 GHG emissions reduction targets and progress"^^xsd:string,
        "Disclosure 102-4 GHG emissions reduction targets and progress 21"^^xsd:string ;
    rso:sourceDocument "GRI 102_ Climate Change 2025.pdf"^^xsd:string ;
    schema1:identifier "Disclosure 102-4 GHG emissions reduction targets a"^^xsd:string ;
    schema1:name "Disclosure 102-4 GHG emissions reduction targets and progress"^^xsd:string,
        "Disclosure 102-4 GHG emissions reduction targets and progress 21"^^xsd:string ;
    schema1:text "Disclosure 102-4 GHG emissions reduction targets and progress 21"^^xsd:string,
        "Disclosure 102-4 GHG emissions reduction targets and progress\\nThe organization shall: REQUIREMENTS\\na.\\nreport short-, medium-, and long-term gross Scope 1, Scope 2, and Scope 3 GHG emissions reduction targets in metric tons of CO ₂ equivalent and as a percentage of base year emissions, where:\\ngross Scope 1, Scope 2, and Scope 3 GHG emissions reduction targets are reported separately or where Scope 1 and Scope 2 GHG emissions are combined; i.\\ngross Scope 1 and Scope 2 GHG emissions reduction targets cover the total Scope 1 and Scope 2 GHG emissions reported under Disclosures 102-5 and 102-6; ii.\\nGHG removals, GHG trades, and avoided GHG emissions are excluded; iii.\\nfor each gross GHG emissions reduction target, report whether biogenic CO ₂ emissions are included in the target; b.\\nfor each gross Scope 2 GHG emissions reduction target, report whether the targets use the location-based or market-based method; c.\\nfor each gross Scope 3 GHG emissions reduction target, list the Scope 3 categories covered by the targets; d.\\nfor each gross GHG emissions reduction target, report the gases covered by the target; e.\\nexplain how the gross GHG emissions reduction targets align with the latest scientific evidence on the effort needed to limit global warming to 1.5°C; f.\\ndescribe its gross GHG emissions reduction target revision policy; g.\\nh.\\nfor each gross GHG emissions reduction target, report the base year, including:\\nthe rationale for choosing it; i.\\nbase year emissions in metric tons of CO 2 equivalent; ii.\\nthe context for any significant changes in emissions that triggered recalculations of base year emissions; iii.\\nthe previously reported base year emissions, if base year emissions are recalculated; iv.\\ni.\\nreport the progress toward each gross GHG emissions reduction target using the inventory method, in metric tons of CO 2 equivalent, and as a percentage of a base year emissions;\\nj.\\nfor each gross GHG emissions reduction target, explain how the progress toward the target was achieved and whether it is due to:\\nreductions as a result of the organization’s initiatives; or i.\\nother factors; ii.\\nreport standards, methodologies, assumptions, and calculation tools used. k.\\nThe GHG emissions reduction targets reported under this disclosure are used to report the targets to achieve the transition plan under 102-1-f ."^^xsd:string .

rso:Disclosure_Disclosure_102_5_Scope_1_GHG_emissions a rso:Disclosure ;
    rso:belongsToTopic rso:Topic_GRI_102_Climate_Change_2025 ;
    rso:originalText "Disclosure 102-5 Scope 1 GHG emissions\\nThe organization shall: REQUIREMENTS\\nreport gross Scope 1 GHG emissions in metric tons of CO ₂ equivalent, and in the calculation\\na.\\ninclude emissions of CO 2 , CH 4 , N 2 O, HFCs, PFCs, SF 6 , and NF 3 ; i.\\ninclude biogenic non-CO 2 GHG emissions produced by combustion or biodegradation of biomass from owned or controlled sources; ii.\\nexclude GHG removals, GHG trades, and avoided emissions; iii.\\nuse the global warming potential (GWP) values based on a 100-year timeframe from the latest IPCC assessment report; iv.\\nprovide a breakdown of gross Scope 1 GHG emissions by CO 2 , CH 4 , N 2 O, HFCs, PFCs, SF 6 , and NF 3 , in metric tons and metric tons of CO 2 equivalent; b.\\nreport biogenic CO ₂ emissions from the combustion or biodegradation of biomass from owned or controlled sources in metric tons, separately from gross Scope 1 GHG emissions;\\nc.\\nd.\\nreport the base year for the calculation, including:\\nthe rationale for choosing it; i.\\nbase year emissions in metric tons of CO 2 equivalent separately for gross Scope 1 GHG emissions and biogenic CO 2 emissions; ii.\\nthe context for any significant changes in emissions that triggered recalculations of base year emissions; iii.\\nthe previously reported base year emissions, if base year emissions are recalculated; iv.\\ne.\\nreport the consolidation approach for Scope 1 GHG emissions that is consistently applied across Scope 1, Scope 2, and Scope 3 GHG emissions, whether equity share, financial control, or operational control;\\nreport standards, methodologies, assumptions, and calculation tools used, including the source of the emission factors used. f.\\nGross Scope 1 GHG emissions include those from energy consumption as reported under 103- 2-a in GRI 103: Energy 2025 ."^^xsd:string ;
    rso:pageEnd 28 ;
    rso:pageStart 25 ;
    rso:requires rso:Requirement_-3891040296556373424 ;
    rso:sectionLabel "Disclosure 102-5 Scope 1 GHG emissions"^^xsd:string ;
    rso:sourceDocument "GRI 102_ Climate Change 2025.pdf"^^xsd:string ;
    schema1:identifier "Disclosure 102-5 Scope 1 GHG emissions"^^xsd:string ;
    schema1:name "Disclosure 102-5 Scope 1 GHG emissions"^^xsd:string ;
    schema1:text "Disclosure 102-5 Scope 1 GHG emissions\\nThe organization shall: REQUIREMENTS\\nreport gross Scope 1 GHG emissions in metric tons of CO ₂ equivalent, and in the calculation\\na.\\ninclude emissions of CO 2 , CH 4 , N 2 O, HFCs, PFCs, SF 6 , and NF 3 ; i.\\ninclude biogenic non-CO 2 GHG emissions produced by combustion or biodegradation of biomass from owned or controlled sources; ii.\\nexclude GHG removals, GHG trades, and avoided emissions; iii.\\nuse the global warming potential (GWP) values based on a 100-year timeframe from the latest IPCC assessment report; iv.\\nprovide a breakdown of gross Scope 1 GHG emissions by CO 2 , CH 4 , N 2 O, HFCs, PFCs, SF 6 , and NF 3 , in metric tons and metric tons of CO 2 equivalent; b.\\nreport biogenic CO ₂ emissions from the combustion or biodegradation of biomass from owned or controlled sources in metric tons, separately from gross Scope 1 GHG emissions;\\nc.\\nd.\\nreport the base year for the calculation, including:\\nthe rationale for choosing it; i.\\nbase year emissions in metric tons of CO 2 equivalent separately for gross Scope 1 GHG emissions and biogenic CO 2 emissions; ii.\\nthe context for any significant changes in emissions that triggered recalculations of base year emissions; iii.\\nthe previously reported base year emissions, if base year emissions are recalculated; iv.\\ne.\\nreport the consolidation approach for Scope 1 GHG emissions that is consistently applied across Scope 1, Scope 2, and Scope 3 GHG emissions, whether equity share, financial control, or operational control;\\nreport standards, methodologies, assumptions, and calculation tools used, including the source of the emission factors used. f.\\nGross Scope 1 GHG emissions include those from energy consumption as reported under 103- 2-a in GRI 103: Energy 2025 ."^^xsd:string .

rso:Disclosure_Disclosure_102_6_Scope_2_GHG_emissions a rso:Disclosure ;
    rso:belongsToTopic rso:Topic_GRI_102_Climate_Change_2025 ;
    rso:originalText "Disclosure 102-6 Scope 2 GHG emissions\\nThe organization shall: REQUIREMENTS\\na.\\nreport gross location-based and, if applicable, market-based Scope 2 GHG emissions in metric tons of CO 2 equivalent, and in the calculation:\\ninclude emissions of CO 2 , CH 4 , and N 2 O; i.\\ninclude biogenic non-CO 2 GHG emissions from electricity use; ii.\\nexclude GHG removals, GHG trades, and avoided emissions; iii.\\nuse the global warming potential (GWP) values based on a 100-year timeframe from the latest IPCC assessment report; iv.\\nprovide a breakdown of gross location-based Scope 2 GHG emissions by CO 2, CH 4 , and N 2 O in metric tons and metric tons of CO 2 equivalent; b.\\nreport location-based and, if applicable, market-based biogenic CO ₂ emissions from electricity use in metric tons, separately from gross Scope 2 GHG emissions; c.\\nd.\\nreport the base year for the calculation, including:\\nthe rationale for choosing it; i.\\nbase year emissions in metric tons of CO ₂ equivalent separately for gross Scope 2 GHG emissions and biogenic CO 2 emissions; ii.\\nthe context for any significant changes in emissions that triggered recalculations of base year emissions; iii.\\nthe previously reported base year emissions, if base year emissions are recalculated; iv.\\ne.\\nreport the consolidation approach for Scope 2 GHG emissions that is consistently applied across Scope 1, Scope 2, and Scope 3 GHG emissions, whether equity share, financial control, or operational control;\\nreport standards, methodologies, assumptions, and calculation tools used, including the source of the emission factors used. f.\\nGross Scope 2 GHG emissions include those from the generation of purchased or acquired electricity, heating, cooling, and steam consumed by an organization reported under 103-2-b in GRI 103: Energy 2025 . For many organizations, Scope 2 GHG emissions from the generation of purchased or acquired electricity can be much greater than Scope 1 GHG emissions."^^xsd:string ;
    rso:pageEnd 31 ;
    rso:pageStart 28 ;
    rso:requires rso:Requirement_2936525716634346325 ;
    rso:sectionLabel "Disclosure 102-6 Scope 2 GHG emissions"^^xsd:string ;
    rso:sourceDocument "GRI 102_ Climate Change 2025.pdf"^^xsd:string ;
    schema1:identifier "Disclosure 102-6 Scope 2 GHG emissions"^^xsd:string ;
    schema1:name "Disclosure 102-6 Scope 2 GHG emissions"^^xsd:string ;
    schema1:text "Disclosure 102-6 Scope 2 GHG emissions\\nThe organization shall: REQUIREMENTS\\na.\\nreport gross location-based and, if applicable, market-based Scope 2 GHG emissions in metric tons of CO 2 equivalent, and in the calculation:\\ninclude emissions of CO 2 , CH 4 , and N 2 O; i.\\ninclude biogenic non-CO 2 GHG emissions from electricity use; ii.\\nexclude GHG removals, GHG trades, and avoided emissions; iii.\\nuse the global warming potential (GWP) values based on a 100-year timeframe from the latest IPCC assessment report; iv.\\nprovide a breakdown of gross location-based Scope 2 GHG emissions by CO 2, CH 4 , and N 2 O in metric tons and metric tons of CO 2 equivalent; b.\\nreport location-based and, if applicable, market-based biogenic CO ₂ emissions from electricity use in metric tons, separately from gross Scope 2 GHG emissions; c.\\nd.\\nreport the base year for the calculation, including:\\nthe rationale for choosing it; i.\\nbase year emissions in metric tons of CO ₂ equivalent separately for gross Scope 2 GHG emissions and biogenic CO 2 emissions; ii.\\nthe context for any significant changes in emissions that triggered recalculations of base year emissions; iii.\\nthe previously reported base year emissions, if base year emissions are recalculated; iv.\\ne.\\nreport the consolidation approach for Scope 2 GHG emissions that is consistently applied across Scope 1, Scope 2, and Scope 3 GHG emissions, whether equity share, financial control, or operational control;\\nreport standards, methodologies, assumptions, and calculation tools used, including the source of the emission factors used. f.\\nGross Scope 2 GHG emissions include those from the generation of purchased or acquired electricity, heating, cooling, and steam consumed by an organization reported under 103-2-b in GRI 103: Energy 2025 . For many organizations, Scope 2 GHG emissions from the generation of purchased or acquired electricity can be much greater than Scope 1 GHG emissions."^^xsd:string .

rso:Disclosure_Disclosure_102_7_Scope_3_GHG_emissions a rso:Disclosure ;
    rso:belongsToTopic rso:Topic_GRI_102_Climate_Change_2025 ;
    rso:originalText "Disclosure 102-7 Scope 3 GHG emissions\\nThe organization shall: REQUIREMENTS\\nreport gross Scope 3 GHG emissions in metric tons of CO ₂ equivalent, and in the calculation:\\na.\\ninclude GHG emissions for each Scope 3 category; i.\\ninclude emissions of CO 2 , CH 4 , N 2 O, HFCs, PFCs, SF 6 , and NF 3 ; ii.\\ninclude biogenic non-CO 2 GHG emissions from the combustion or biodegradation of biomass in the upstream and downstream value chain; iii.\\nexclude GHG removals, GHG trades, and avoided emissions; iv.\\nuse the global warming potential (GWP) values based on a 100-year timeframe from the latest IPCC assessment report; v.\\nprovide a breakdown of gross Scope 3 GHG emissions by each of the 15 Scope 3 categories in metric tons of CO 2 equivalent; b.\\nreport biogenic CO ₂ emissions from the combustion or biodegradation of biomass in the upstream and downstream value chain in metric tons, separately from gross Scope 3 GHG emissions, and a breakdown of this total by each of the 15 Scope 3 categories;\\nc.\\nd.\\nreport the base year for the calculation, including:\\nthe rationale for choosing it; i.\\nbase year emissions in metric tons of CO 2 equivalent separately for gross Scope 3 GHG emissions and biogenic CO 2 emissions; ii.\\nthe context for any significant changes in emissions that triggered recalculations of base year emissions; iii.\\nthe previously reported base year emissions, if base year emissions are recalculated; iv.\\ne.\\nreport the consolidation approach for Scope 3 GHG emissions that is consistently applied across Scope 1, Scope 2, and Scope 3 GHG emissions, whether equity share, financial control, or operational control;\\nreport standards, methodologies, assumptions, and calculation tools used, including the sources of the emission factors used. f.\\nScope 3 GHG emissions are all indirect GHG emissions (not included in Scope 2) that occur in the organization’s upstream and downstream value chain."^^xsd:string ;
    rso:pageEnd 34 ;
    rso:pageStart 31 ;
    rso:requires rso:Requirement_-3765219973467961704 ;
    rso:sectionLabel "Disclosure 102-7 Scope 3 GHG emissions"^^xsd:string ;
    rso:sourceDocument "GRI 102_ Climate Change 2025.pdf"^^xsd:string ;
    schema1:identifier "Disclosure 102-7 Scope 3 GHG emissions"^^xsd:string ;
    schema1:name "Disclosure 102-7 Scope 3 GHG emissions"^^xsd:string ;
    schema1:text "Disclosure 102-7 Scope 3 GHG emissions\\nThe organization shall: REQUIREMENTS\\nreport gross Scope 3 GHG emissions in metric tons of CO ₂ equivalent, and in the calculation:\\na.\\ninclude GHG emissions for each Scope 3 category; i.\\ninclude emissions of CO 2 , CH 4 , N 2 O, HFCs, PFCs, SF 6 , and NF 3 ; ii.\\ninclude biogenic non-CO 2 GHG emissions from the combustion or biodegradation of biomass in the upstream and downstream value chain; iii.\\nexclude GHG removals, GHG trades, and avoided emissions; iv.\\nuse the global warming potential (GWP) values based on a 100-year timeframe from the latest IPCC assessment report; v.\\nprovide a breakdown of gross Scope 3 GHG emissions by each of the 15 Scope 3 categories in metric tons of CO 2 equivalent; b.\\nreport biogenic CO ₂ emissions from the combustion or biodegradation of biomass in the upstream and downstream value chain in metric tons, separately from gross Scope 3 GHG emissions, and a breakdown of this total by each of the 15 Scope 3 categories;\\nc.\\nd.\\nreport the base year for the calculation, including:\\nthe rationale for choosing it; i.\\nbase year emissions in metric tons of CO 2 equivalent separately for gross Scope 3 GHG emissions and biogenic CO 2 emissions; ii.\\nthe context for any significant changes in emissions that triggered recalculations of base year emissions; iii.\\nthe previously reported base year emissions, if base year emissions are recalculated; iv.\\ne.\\nreport the consolidation approach for Scope 3 GHG emissions that is consistently applied across Scope 1, Scope 2, and Scope 3 GHG emissions, whether equity share, financial control, or operational control;\\nreport standards, methodologies, assumptions, and calculation tools used, including the sources of the emission factors used. f.\\nScope 3 GHG emissions are all indirect GHG emissions (not included in Scope 2) that occur in the organization’s upstream and downstream value chain."^^xsd:string .

rso:Disclosure_Disclosure_102_8_GHG_emissions_intensity a rso:Disclosure ;
    rso:belongsToTopic rso:Topic_GRI_102_Climate_Change_2025 ;
    rso:originalText "Disclosure 102-8 GHG emissions intensity\\nThe organization shall: REQUIREMENTS\\na.\\nreport GHG emissions intensity ratio(s), including the gross GHG emissions in metric tons of CO ₂ equivalent (the numerator) and the organization-specific metric (the denominator) chosen to calculate the ratio(s);\\nreport the scope(s) of GHG emissions included in the intensity ratio(s), whether Scope 1, Scope 2, or Scope 3. b.\\nGHG emissions intensity ratios are obtained by dividing the organization’s gross GHG emissions (the numerator) by an organization-specific metric (the denominator). Many organizations track environmental performance with intensity ratio(s)."^^xsd:string ;
    rso:pageEnd 35 ;
    rso:pageStart 34 ;
    rso:requires rso:Requirement_2479117435396769603 ;
    rso:sectionLabel "Disclosure 102-8 GHG emissions intensity"^^xsd:string ;
    rso:sourceDocument "GRI 102_ Climate Change 2025.pdf"^^xsd:string ;
    schema1:identifier "Disclosure 102-8 GHG emissions intensity"^^xsd:string ;
    schema1:name "Disclosure 102-8 GHG emissions intensity"^^xsd:string ;
    schema1:text "Disclosure 102-8 GHG emissions intensity\\nThe organization shall: REQUIREMENTS\\na.\\nreport GHG emissions intensity ratio(s), including the gross GHG emissions in metric tons of CO ₂ equivalent (the numerator) and the organization-specific metric (the denominator) chosen to calculate the ratio(s);\\nreport the scope(s) of GHG emissions included in the intensity ratio(s), whether Scope 1, Scope 2, or Scope 3. b.\\nGHG emissions intensity ratios are obtained by dividing the organization’s gross GHG emissions (the numerator) by an organization-specific metric (the denominator). Many organizations track environmental performance with intensity ratio(s)."^^xsd:string .

rso:Disclosure_Disclosure_102_9_GHG_removals_in_the_value_chain a rso:Disclosure ;
    rso:belongsToTopic rso:Topic_GRI_102_Climate_Change_2025 ;
    rso:originalText "Disclosure 102-9 GHG removals in the value chain\\nThe organization shall: REQUIREMENTS\\nreport the total Scope 1 GHG removals in metric tons of CO ₂ equivalent, excluding any GHG trades, and a breakdown of this total by each storage pool; a.\\nfor each type of storage pool, describe how quality criteria are monitored to manage the risk of non-permanence; b.\\nreport the intended use of GHG removals; c.\\nd.\\ndescribe the impacts on people and the environment from its Scope 1 GHG removals and the actions taken to manage them, including for:\\nworkers, local communities, and Indigenous Peoples; i.\\nbiodiversity; ii.\\nreport standards, methodologies, assumptions, and calculation tools used. e.\\nThis disclosure aims to increase transparency regarding the organization’s GHG removals."^^xsd:string ;
    rso:pageEnd 37 ;
    rso:pageStart 35 ;
    rso:requires rso:Requirement_-6868122415516816571 ;
    rso:sectionLabel "Disclosure 102-9 GHG removals in the value chain"^^xsd:string ;
    rso:sourceDocument "GRI 102_ Climate Change 2025.pdf"^^xsd:string ;
    schema1:identifier "Disclosure 102-9 GHG removals in the value chain"^^xsd:string ;
    schema1:name "Disclosure 102-9 GHG removals in the value chain"^^xsd:string ;
    schema1:text "Disclosure 102-9 GHG removals in the value chain\\nThe organization shall: REQUIREMENTS\\nreport the total Scope 1 GHG removals in metric tons of CO ₂ equivalent, excluding any GHG trades, and a breakdown of this total by each storage pool; a.\\nfor each type of storage pool, describe how quality criteria are monitored to manage the risk of non-permanence; b.\\nreport the intended use of GHG removals; c.\\nd.\\ndescribe the impacts on people and the environment from its Scope 1 GHG removals and the actions taken to manage them, including for:\\nworkers, local communities, and Indigenous Peoples; i.\\nbiodiversity; ii.\\nreport standards, methodologies, assumptions, and calculation tools used. e.\\nThis disclosure aims to increase transparency regarding the organization’s GHG removals."^^xsd:string .

rso:Disclosure_Disclosure_103_1_Energy_policies_and_commitments a rso:Disclosure ;
    rso:belongsToTopic rso:Topic_GRI_103_Energy_2025 ;
    rso:originalText "Disclosure 103-1 Energy policies and commitments\\nThe organization shall: REQUIREMENTS\\na.\\ndescribe how its energy-related policies and commitments contribute to energy consumption reduction, energy efficiency, and the transition to renewable energy sources;\\ndescribe the impacts on the economy, environment, and people that may result from its energy consumption and the transition to renewable energy sources. b.\\nGuidance to 103-1-a This requirement covers policies and commitments that apply to the organization’s activities and its upstream and downstream value chain."^^xsd:string ;
    rso:pageEnd 8 ;
    rso:pageStart 7 ;
    rso:requires rso:Requirement_-6613517553719183492 ;
    rso:sectionLabel "Disclosure 103-1 Energy policies and commitments"^^xsd:string ;
    rso:sourceDocument "GRI 103_ Energy 2025.pdf"^^xsd:string ;
    schema1:identifier "Disclosure 103-1 Energy policies and commitments"^^xsd:string ;
    schema1:name "Disclosure 103-1 Energy policies and commitments"^^xsd:string ;
    schema1:text "Disclosure 103-1 Energy policies and commitments\\nThe organization shall: REQUIREMENTS\\na.\\ndescribe how its energy-related policies and commitments contribute to energy consumption reduction, energy efficiency, and the transition to renewable energy sources;\\ndescribe the impacts on the economy, environment, and people that may result from its energy consumption and the transition to renewable energy sources. b.\\nGuidance to 103-1-a This requirement covers policies and commitments that apply to the organization’s activities and its upstream and downstream value chain."^^xsd:string .

rso:Disclosure_Disclosure_103_2_Energy_consumption_and_self_gener a rso:Disclosure ;
    rso:belongsToTopic rso:Topic_GRI_103_Energy_2025 ;
    rso:originalText "Disclosure 103-2 Energy consumption and self-generation within the organization 9"^^xsd:string,
        "Disclosure 103-2 Energy consumption and self-generation within the organization\\nThe organization shall: REQUIREMENTS\\na.\\nreport total fuel consumption within the organization in joules, watt-hours, or multiples, and a breakdown of this total by:\\nrenewable and non-renewable energy sources; i.\\neach activity in which the fuel is consumed for each renewable and non-renewable energy source; ii.\\nb.\\nreport total purchased electricity, heating, cooling, and steam consumption within the organization in joules, watt-hours, or multiples, and a breakdown of this total by:\\nrenewable and non-renewable energy sources; i.\\nelectricity, heating, cooling, and steam consumption for each renewable and non- renewable energy source; ii.\\nc.\\nreport total self-generated renewable electricity, heating, cooling, and steam consumption within the organization in joules, watt-hours, or multiples, and a breakdown of this total by electricity, heating, cooling, and steam consumption for each activity in which it is consumed for each renewable energy source;\\nd.\\nreport total self-generated electricity, heating, cooling, and steam sold in joules, watt- hours, or multiples, and a breakdown of this total by:\\nrenewable and non-renewable energy sources; i.\\nelectricity, heating, cooling, and steam sold for each renewable and non- renewable energy source; ii.\\ne.\\nreport whether contractual instruments are used to disclose information on purchased electricity, heating, cooling, and steam consumption, and if so, describe how the contractual instruments adhere to quality criteria to ensure accuracy and consistency;\\nreport standards, methodologies, assumptions, and calculation tools used, including the source of the conversion factors used. f.\\nThroughout this guidance, electricity, heating, cooling, and steam are collectively referred to as ‘electricity’ in alignment with the GHG Protocol Scope 2 Guidance [ 5 ]. In other frameworks, ‘electricity’ can be referred to as ‘non-fuel’."^^xsd:string ;
    rso:pageEnd 3,
        13 ;
    rso:pageStart 3,
        9 ;
    rso:requires rso:Requirement_-7780957563181553695 ;
    rso:sectionLabel "Disclosure 103-2 Energy consumption and self-generation within the organization"^^xsd:string,
        "Disclosure 103-2 Energy consumption and self-generation within the organization 9"^^xsd:string ;
    rso:sourceDocument "GRI 103_ Energy 2025.pdf"^^xsd:string ;
    schema1:identifier "Disclosure 103-2 Energy consumption and self-gener"^^xsd:string ;
    schema1:name "Disclosure 103-2 Energy consumption and self-generation within the organization"^^xsd:string,
        "Disclosure 103-2 Energy consumption and self-generation within the organization 9"^^xsd:string ;
    schema1:text "Disclosure 103-2 Energy consumption and self-generation within the organization 9"^^xsd:string,
        "Disclosure 103-2 Energy consumption and self-generation within the organization\\nThe organization shall: REQUIREMENTS\\na.\\nreport total fuel consumption within the organization in joules, watt-hours, or multiples, and a breakdown of this total by:\\nrenewable and non-renewable energy sources; i.\\neach activity in which the fuel is consumed for each renewable and non-renewable energy source; ii.\\nb.\\nreport total purchased electricity, heating, cooling, and steam consumption within the organization in joules, watt-hours, or multiples, and a breakdown of this total by:\\nrenewable and non-renewable energy sources; i.\\nelectricity, heating, cooling, and steam consumption for each renewable and non- renewable energy source; ii.\\nc.\\nreport total self-generated renewable electricity, heating, cooling, and steam consumption within the organization in joules, watt-hours, or multiples, and a breakdown of this total by electricity, heating, cooling, and steam consumption for each activity in which it is consumed for each renewable energy source;\\nd.\\nreport total self-generated electricity, heating, cooling, and steam sold in joules, watt- hours, or multiples, and a breakdown of this total by:\\nrenewable and non-renewable energy sources; i.\\nelectricity, heating, cooling, and steam sold for each renewable and non- renewable energy source; ii.\\ne.\\nreport whether contractual instruments are used to disclose information on purchased electricity, heating, cooling, and steam consumption, and if so, describe how the contractual instruments adhere to quality criteria to ensure accuracy and consistency;\\nreport standards, methodologies, assumptions, and calculation tools used, including the source of the conversion factors used. f.\\nThroughout this guidance, electricity, heating, cooling, and steam are collectively referred to as ‘electricity’ in alignment with the GHG Protocol Scope 2 Guidance [ 5 ]. In other frameworks, ‘electricity’ can be referred to as ‘non-fuel’."^^xsd:string .

rso:Disclosure_Disclosure_103_3_Upstream_and_downstream_energy_co a rso:Disclosure ;
    rso:belongsToTopic rso:Topic_GRI_103_Energy_2025 ;
    rso:originalText "Disclosure 103-3 Upstream and downstream energy consumption 13"^^xsd:string,
        "Disclosure 103-3 Upstream and downstream energy consumption\\nThe organization shall: REQUIREMENTS\\na.\\nreport total significant energy consumption in its upstream and downstream value chain in joules, watt-hours, or multiples, and list the upstream and downstream categories in which significant energy consumption occurs;\\nreport standards, methodologies, assumptions, and calculation tools used, including the source of the conversion factors used. b.\\nThis disclosure covers energy consumption from activities outside the organization and includes the upstream and downstream value chain."^^xsd:string ;
    rso:pageEnd 3,
        15 ;
    rso:pageStart 3,
        13 ;
    rso:requires rso:Requirement_5037093403330450680 ;
    rso:sectionLabel "Disclosure 103-3 Upstream and downstream energy consumption"^^xsd:string,
        "Disclosure 103-3 Upstream and downstream energy consumption 13"^^xsd:string ;
    rso:sourceDocument "GRI 103_ Energy 2025.pdf"^^xsd:string ;
    schema1:identifier "Disclosure 103-3 Upstream and downstream energy co"^^xsd:string ;
    schema1:name "Disclosure 103-3 Upstream and downstream energy consumption"^^xsd:string,
        "Disclosure 103-3 Upstream and downstream energy consumption 13"^^xsd:string ;
    schema1:text "Disclosure 103-3 Upstream and downstream energy consumption 13"^^xsd:string,
        "Disclosure 103-3 Upstream and downstream energy consumption\\nThe organization shall: REQUIREMENTS\\na.\\nreport total significant energy consumption in its upstream and downstream value chain in joules, watt-hours, or multiples, and list the upstream and downstream categories in which significant energy consumption occurs;\\nreport standards, methodologies, assumptions, and calculation tools used, including the source of the conversion factors used. b.\\nThis disclosure covers energy consumption from activities outside the organization and includes the upstream and downstream value chain."^^xsd:string .

rso:Disclosure_Disclosure_103_4_Energy_intensity a rso:Disclosure ;
    rso:belongsToTopic rso:Topic_GRI_103_Energy_2025 ;
    rso:originalText "Disclosure 103-4 Energy intensity\\nThe organization shall: REQUIREMENTS\\na.\\nreport energy intensity ratio(s), including the energy consumption in joules, watt-hours, or multiples (the numerator) and the organization-specific metric (the denominator) chosen to calculate the ratio(s);\\nreport whether the energy intensity ratio(s) include energy consumption within the organization, in its upstream and downstream value chain, or both; b.\\nreport the types of energy consumption included in the energy intensity ratio(s), whether fuel, electricity, heating, cooling, or steam. c.\\nEnergy intensity ratios are obtained by dividing the energy consumption (the numerator) by an organization-specific metric (the denominator). Many organizations track environmental performance with intensity ratio(s)."^^xsd:string ;
    rso:pageEnd 17 ;
    rso:pageStart 15 ;
    rso:requires rso:Requirement_-8401216917174818540 ;
    rso:sectionLabel "Disclosure 103-4 Energy intensity"^^xsd:string ;
    rso:sourceDocument "GRI 103_ Energy 2025.pdf"^^xsd:string ;
    schema1:identifier "Disclosure 103-4 Energy intensity"^^xsd:string ;
    schema1:name "Disclosure 103-4 Energy intensity"^^xsd:string ;
    schema1:text "Disclosure 103-4 Energy intensity\\nThe organization shall: REQUIREMENTS\\na.\\nreport energy intensity ratio(s), including the energy consumption in joules, watt-hours, or multiples (the numerator) and the organization-specific metric (the denominator) chosen to calculate the ratio(s);\\nreport whether the energy intensity ratio(s) include energy consumption within the organization, in its upstream and downstream value chain, or both; b.\\nreport the types of energy consumption included in the energy intensity ratio(s), whether fuel, electricity, heating, cooling, or steam. c.\\nEnergy intensity ratios are obtained by dividing the energy consumption (the numerator) by an organization-specific metric (the denominator). Many organizations track environmental performance with intensity ratio(s)."^^xsd:string .

rso:Disclosure_Disclosure_103_5_Reduction_in_energy_consumption a rso:Disclosure ;
    rso:belongsToTopic rso:Topic_GRI_103_Energy_2025 ;
    rso:originalText "Disclosure 103-5 Reduction in energy consumption\\nThe organization shall: REQUIREMENTS\\na.\\nreport the reduction in energy consumption achieved in joules, watt-hours, or multiples, including whether and how it is due to:\\nreductions from the organization’s conservation and efficiency initiatives; i.\\nother factors; ii.\\nreport the types of energy consumption included in the reduction, whether fuel, electricity, heating, cooling, or steam; b.\\nc.\\nreport whether the reduction in energy consumption was achieved within the organization, in its upstream and downstream value chain, or both, and list the upstream and downstream categories in which reduction was achieved;\\nd.\\nreport whether the reduction in energy consumption is estimated, modeled, or sourced from direct measurements and, if applicable, the estimations or modeling methods used;\\ne.\\nreport the base year or baseline for calculating the reduction in energy consumption, including:\\nthe rationale for choosing it; i.\\nenergy consumption in the base year or baseline; ii.\\nreport standards, methodologies, assumptions, and calculation tools used. f.\\nReductions in energy consumption can be related to target setting. For further information on energy target reporting, see Guidance to 103-1-a ."^^xsd:string ;
    rso:pageEnd 29 ;
    rso:pageStart 17 ;
    rso:requires rso:Requirement_-3288099168552378632 ;
    rso:sectionLabel "Disclosure 103-5 Reduction in energy consumption"^^xsd:string ;
    rso:sourceDocument "GRI 103_ Energy 2025.pdf"^^xsd:string ;
    schema1:identifier "Disclosure 103-5 Reduction in energy consumption"^^xsd:string ;
    schema1:name "Disclosure 103-5 Reduction in energy consumption"^^xsd:string ;
    schema1:text "Disclosure 103-5 Reduction in energy consumption\\nThe organization shall: REQUIREMENTS\\na.\\nreport the reduction in energy consumption achieved in joules, watt-hours, or multiples, including whether and how it is due to:\\nreductions from the organization’s conservation and efficiency initiatives; i.\\nother factors; ii.\\nreport the types of energy consumption included in the reduction, whether fuel, electricity, heating, cooling, or steam; b.\\nc.\\nreport whether the reduction in energy consumption was achieved within the organization, in its upstream and downstream value chain, or both, and list the upstream and downstream categories in which reduction was achieved;\\nd.\\nreport whether the reduction in energy consumption is estimated, modeled, or sourced from direct measurements and, if applicable, the estimations or modeling methods used;\\ne.\\nreport the base year or baseline for calculating the reduction in energy consumption, including:\\nthe rationale for choosing it; i.\\nenergy consumption in the base year or baseline; ii.\\nreport standards, methodologies, assumptions, and calculation tools used. f.\\nReductions in energy consumption can be related to target setting. For further information on energy target reporting, see Guidance to 103-1-a ."^^xsd:string .

rso:Disclosure_Disclosure_203_2_Significant_indirect_economic_imp a rso:Disclosure ;
    rso:belongsToTopic rso:Topic_GRI_203_Indirect_Economic_Impacts_2016_3 ;
    rso:originalText "Disclosure 203-2 Significant indirect economic impacts 9\\nGRI 203: Indirect Economic Impacts 2016 4\\nIntroduction\\nGRI 203: Indirect Economic Impacts 2016 contains disclosures for organizations to report information about their indirect economic impacts, and how they manage these impacts.\\nThe Standard is structured as follows:\\nSection 1 contains a requirement, which provides information about how the organization manages its indirect economic impacts. •\\nSection 2 contains two disclosures, which provide information about the organization’s indirect economic impacts. • The Glossary contains defined terms with a specific meaning when used in the GRI Standards. The terms are underlined in the text of the GRI Standards and linked to the definitions. •\\nThe rest of the Introduction section provides a background on the topic, an overview of the system of GRI Standards and further information on using this Standard.\\nBackground on the topic This Standard addresses an organization’s indirect economic impacts, including impacts of an organization’s infrastructure investments and services supported.\\nAn economic impact can be defined as a change in the productive potential of the economy that has an influence on a community’s or stakeholder’s well-being and longer-term prospects for development. Indirect economic impacts are the additional consequences of the direct impact of financial transactions and the flow of money between an organization and its stakeholders.\\nIndirect economic impacts can be monetary or non-monetary, and are particularly important to assess in relation to local communities and regional economies.\\nSystem of GRI Standards This Standard is part of the GRI Sustainability Reporting Standards (GRI Standards). The GRI Standards enable an organization to report information about its most significant impacts on the economy, environment, and people, including impacts on their human rights, and how it manages these impacts.\\nThe GRI Standards are structured as a system of interrelated standards that are organized into three series: GRI Universal Standards, GRI Sector Standards, and GRI Topic Standards (see Figure 1 in this Standard).\\nUniversal Standards: GRI 1, GRI 2 and GRI 3 GRI 1: Foundation 2021 specifies the requirements that the organization must comply with to report in accordance with the GRI Standards. The organization begins using the GRI Standards by consulting GRI 1 .\\nGRI 2: General Disclosures 2021 contains disclosures that the organization uses to provide information about its reporting practices and other organizational details, such as its activities, governance, and policies.\\nGRI 3: Material Topics 2021 provides guidance on how to determine material topics. It also contains disclosures that the organization uses to report information about its process of determining material topics, its list of material topics, and how it manages each topic.\\nSector Standards The Sector Standards provide information for organizations about their likely material topics. The organization uses the Sector Standards that apply to its sectors when determining its material topics and when determining what to report for each material topic.\\nTopic Standards The Topic Standards contain disclosures that the organization uses to report information about its impacts in relation to particular topics. The organization uses the Topic Standards according to the list of material topics it has determined using GRI 3 .\\nGRI 203: Indirect Economic Impacts 2016 5\\nFigure 1. GRI Standards: Universal, Sector and Topic Standards\\nGRI Standards\\nSector Standards Universal Standards Topic Standards\\nRequirements and principles for using the GRI Standards\\nDisclosures about the reporting organization\\nDisclosures and guidance about the organization's material topics\\nApply all three Universal Standards to your reporting\\nUse the Sector Standards that\\nSelect Topic Standards to report\\napply to your sectors\\nspecific information on your\\nmaterial topics\\nUsing this Standard This Standard can be used by any organization – regardless of size, type, sector, geographic location, or reporting experience – to report information about its indirect economic impacts.\\nAn organization reporting in accordance with the GRI Standards is required to report the following disclosures if it has determined indirect economic impacts to be a material topic:"^^xsd:string,
        "Disclosure 203-2 Significant indirect economic impacts\\nThe reporting organization shall report the following information: REQUIREMENTS\\nExamples of significant identified indirect economic impacts of the organization, including positive and negative impacts. a.\\nb.\\nSignificance of the indirect economic impacts in the context of external benchmarks and stakeholder priorities, such as national and international standards, protocols, and policy agendas.\\nGuidance for Disclosure 203-2 This disclosure concerns the spectrum of indirect economic impacts that an organization can have on its stakeholders and the economy."^^xsd:string ;
    rso:pageEnd 5,
        11 ;
    rso:pageStart 3,
        9 ;
    rso:requires rso:Requirement_-5787370671646401107 ;
    rso:sectionLabel "Disclosure 203-2 Significant indirect economic impacts"^^xsd:string,
        "Disclosure 203-2 Significant indirect economic impacts 9"^^xsd:string ;
    rso:sourceDocument "GRI 203_ Indirect Economic Impacts 2016.pdf"^^xsd:string ;
    schema1:identifier "Disclosure 203-2 Significant indirect economic imp"^^xsd:string ;
    schema1:name "Disclosure 203-2 Significant indirect economic impacts"^^xsd:string,
        "Disclosure 203-2 Significant indirect economic impacts 9"^^xsd:string ;
    schema1:text "Disclosure 203-2 Significant indirect economic impacts 9\\nGRI 203: Indirect Economic Impacts 2016 4\\nIntroduction\\nGRI 203: Indirect Economic Impacts 2016 contains disclosures for organizations to report information about their indirect economic impacts, and how they manage these impacts.\\nThe Standard is structured as follows:\\nSection 1 contains a requirement, which provides information about how the organization manages its indirect economic impacts. •\\nSection 2 contains two disclosures, which provide information about the organization’s indirect economic impacts. • The Glossary contains defined terms with a specific meaning when used in the GRI Standards. The terms are underlined in the text of the GRI Standards and linked to the definitions. •\\nThe rest of the Introduction section provides a background on the topic, an overview of the system of GRI Standards and further information on using this Standard.\\nBackground on the topic This Standard addresses an organization’s indirect economic impacts, including impacts of an organization’s infrastructure investments and services supported.\\nAn economic impact can be defined as a change in the productive potential of the economy that has an influence on a community’s or stakeholder’s well-being and longer-term prospects for development. Indirect economic impacts are the additional consequences of the direct impact of financial transactions and the flow of money between an organization and its stakeholders.\\nIndirect economic impacts can be monetary or non-monetary, and are particularly important to assess in relation to local communities and regional economies.\\nSystem of GRI Standards This Standard is part of the GRI Sustainability Reporting Standards (GRI Standards). The GRI Standards enable an organization to report information about its most significant impacts on the economy, environment, and people, including impacts on their human rights, and how it manages these impacts.\\nThe GRI Standards are structured as a system of interrelated standards that are organized into three series: GRI Universal Standards, GRI Sector Standards, and GRI Topic Standards (see Figure 1 in this Standard).\\nUniversal Standards: GRI 1, GRI 2 and GRI 3 GRI 1: Foundation 2021 specifies the requirements that the organization must comply with to report in accordance with the GRI Standards. The organization begins using the GRI Standards by consulting GRI 1 .\\nGRI 2: General Disclosures 2021 contains disclosures that the organization uses to provide information about its reporting practices and other organizational details, such as its activities, governance, and policies.\\nGRI 3: Material Topics 2021 provides guidance on how to determine material topics. It also contains disclosures that the organization uses to report information about its process of determining material topics, its list of material topics, and how it manages each topic.\\nSector Standards The Sector Standards provide information for organizations about their likely material topics. The organization uses the Sector Standards that apply to its sectors when determining its material topics and when determining what to report for each material topic.\\nTopic Standards The Topic Standards contain disclosures that the organization uses to report information about its impacts in relation to particular topics. The organization uses the Topic Standards according to the list of material topics it has determined using GRI 3 .\\nGRI 203: Indirect Economic Impacts 2016 5\\nFigure 1. GRI Standards: Universal, Sector and Topic Standards\\nGRI Standards\\nSector Standards Universal Standards Topic Standards\\nRequirements and principles for using the GRI Standards\\nDisclosures about the reporting organization\\nDisclosures and guidance about the organization's material topics\\nApply all three Universal Standards to your reporting\\nUse the Sector Standards that\\nSelect Topic Standards to report\\napply to your sectors\\nspecific information on your\\nmaterial topics\\nUsing this Standard This Standard can be used by any organization – regardless of size, type, sector, geographic location, or reporting experience – to report information about its indirect economic impacts.\\nAn organization reporting in accordance with the GRI Standards is required to report the following disclosures if it has determined indirect economic impacts to be a material topic:"^^xsd:string,
        "Disclosure 203-2 Significant indirect economic impacts\\nThe reporting organization shall report the following information: REQUIREMENTS\\nExamples of significant identified indirect economic impacts of the organization, including positive and negative impacts. a.\\nb.\\nSignificance of the indirect economic impacts in the context of external benchmarks and stakeholder priorities, such as national and international standards, protocols, and policy agendas.\\nGuidance for Disclosure 203-2 This disclosure concerns the spectrum of indirect economic impacts that an organization can have on its stakeholders and the economy."^^xsd:string .

rso:Disclosure_Disclosure_205_1_Operations_assessed_for_risks_rel a rso:Disclosure ;
    rso:belongsToTopic rso:Topic_GRI_205_Anti_corruption_2016_3 ;
    rso:originalText "Disclosure 205-1 Operations assessed for risks related to corruption 8"^^xsd:string,
        "Disclosure 205-1 Operations assessed for risks related to corruption\\nThe reporting organization shall report the following information: REQUIREMENTS\\nTotal number and percentage of operations assessed for risks related to corruption. a.\\nSignificant risks related to corruption identified through the risk assessment. b.\\nGuidance for Disclosure 205-1 This disclosure can include a risk assessment focused on corruption or the inclusion of corruption as a risk factor in overall risk assessments."^^xsd:string ;
    rso:pageEnd 3,
        9 ;
    rso:pageStart 3,
        8 ;
    rso:requires rso:Requirement_-890215898363684526 ;
    rso:sectionLabel "Disclosure 205-1 Operations assessed for risks related to corruption"^^xsd:string,
        "Disclosure 205-1 Operations assessed for risks related to corruption 8"^^xsd:string ;
    rso:sourceDocument "GRI 205_ Anti-corruption 2016.pdf"^^xsd:string ;
    schema1:identifier "Disclosure 205-1 Operations assessed for risks rel"^^xsd:string ;
    schema1:name "Disclosure 205-1 Operations assessed for risks related to corruption"^^xsd:string,
        "Disclosure 205-1 Operations assessed for risks related to corruption 8"^^xsd:string ;
    schema1:text "Disclosure 205-1 Operations assessed for risks related to corruption 8"^^xsd:string,
        "Disclosure 205-1 Operations assessed for risks related to corruption\\nThe reporting organization shall report the following information: REQUIREMENTS\\nTotal number and percentage of operations assessed for risks related to corruption. a.\\nSignificant risks related to corruption identified through the risk assessment. b.\\nGuidance for Disclosure 205-1 This disclosure can include a risk assessment focused on corruption or the inclusion of corruption as a risk factor in overall risk assessments."^^xsd:string .

rso:Disclosure_Disclosure_205_3_Confirmed_incidents_of_corruption a rso:Disclosure ;
    rso:belongsToTopic rso:Topic_GRI_205_Anti_corruption_2016_3 ;
    rso:originalText "Disclosure 205-3 Confirmed incidents of corruption and actions taken 10\\nGRI 205: Anti-corruption 2016 4\\nIntroduction\\nGRI 205: Anti-corruption 2016 contains disclosures for organizations to report information about their corruption- related impacts, and how they manage these impacts.\\nThe Standard is structured as follows:\\nSection 1 contains a requirement, which provides information about how the organization manages its corruption- related impacts. •\\nSection 2 contains three disclosures, which provide information about the organization’s corruption-related impacts. •\\nThe Glossary contains defined terms with a specific meaning when used in the GRI Standards. The terms are underlined in the text of the GRI Standards and linked to the definitions. •\\nThe Bibliography lists authoritative intergovernmental instruments and additional references used in developing this Standard. •\\nThe rest of the Introduction section provides a background on the topic, an overview of the system of GRI Standards and further information on using this Standard.\\nBackground on the topic This Standard addresses the topic of anti-corruption. In this Standard, corruption is understood to include practices such as bribery, facilitation payments, fraud, extortion, collusion, and money laundering; the offer or receipt of gifts, loans, fees, rewards, or other advantages as an inducement to do something that is dishonest, illegal, or represents a breach of trust. It can also include practices such as embezzlement, trading in influence, abuse of function, illicit enrichment, concealment, and obstructing justice.\\nCorruption is broadly linked to negative impacts, such as poverty in transition economies, damage to the environment, abuse of human rights, abuse of democracy, misallocation of investments, and undermining the rule of law. Organizations are expected by the marketplace, international norms, and stakeholders to demonstrate their adherence to integrity, governance, and responsible business practices.\\nThese concepts are covered in key instruments of the Organisation for Economic Co-operation and Development and the United Nations: see the Bibliography .\\nSystem of GRI Standards This Standard is part of the GRI Sustainability Reporting Standards (GRI Standards). The GRI Standards enable an organization to report information about its most significant impacts on the economy, environment, and people, including impacts on their human rights, and how it manages these impacts.\\nThe GRI Standards are structured as a system of interrelated standards that are organized into three series: GRI Universal Standards, GRI Sector Standards, and GRI Topic Standards (see Figure 1 in this Standard).\\nUniversal Standards: GRI 1, GRI 2 and GRI 3 GRI 1: Foundation 2021 specifies the requirements that the organization must comply with to report in accordance with the GRI Standards. The organization begins using the GRI Standards by consulting GRI 1 .\\nGRI 2: General Disclosures 2021 contains disclosures that the organization uses to provide information about its reporting practices and other organizational details, such as its activities, governance, and policies.\\nGRI 3: Material Topics 2021 provides guidance on how to determine material topics. It also contains disclosures that the organization uses to report information about its process of determining material topics, its list of material topics, and how it manages each topic.\\nSector Standards The Sector Standards provide information for organizations about their likely material topics. The organization uses the Sector Standards that apply to its sectors when determining its material topics and when determining what to report for each material topic.\\nTopic Standards The Topic Standards contain disclosures that the organization uses to report information about its impacts in relation to particular topics. The organization uses the Topic Standards according to the list of material topics it has determined using GRI 3 .\\nGRI 205: Anti-corruption 2016 5\\nFigure 1. GRI Standards: Universal, Sector and Topic Standards\\nGRI Standards\\nSector Standards Universal Standards Topic Standards\\nRequirements and principles for using the GRI Standards\\nDisclosures about the reporting organization\\nDisclosures and guidance about the organization's material topics\\nApply all three Universal Standards to your reporting\\nUse the Sector Standards that\\nSelect Topic Standards to report\\napply to your sectors\\nspecific information on your\\nmaterial topics\\nUsing this Standard This Standard can be used by any organization – regardless of size, type, sector, geographic location, or reporting experience – to report information about its corruption-related impacts.\\nAn organization reporting in accordance with the GRI Standards is required to report the following disclosures if it has determined anti-corruption to be a material topic:"^^xsd:string,
        "Disclosure 205-3 Confirmed incidents of corruption and actions taken\\nThe reporting organization shall report the following information: REQUIREMENTS\\nTotal number and nature of confirmed incidents of corruption. a.\\nTotal number of confirmed incidents in which employees were dismissed or disciplined for corruption. b.\\nTotal number of confirmed incidents when contracts with business partners were terminated or not renewed due to violations related to corruption. c.\\nPublic legal cases regarding corruption brought against the organization or its employees during the reporting period and the outcomes of such cases. d.\\nGuidance for Disclosure 205-3 For stakeholders, there is an interest in both the occurrence of incidents and an organization’s response to the incidents. Public legal cases regarding corruption can include current public investigations, prosecutions, or closed cases."^^xsd:string ;
    rso:pageEnd 5,
        16 ;
    rso:pageStart 3,
        10 ;
    rso:requires rso:Requirement_8249752634472733823 ;
    rso:sectionLabel "Disclosure 205-3 Confirmed incidents of corruption and actions taken"^^xsd:string,
        "Disclosure 205-3 Confirmed incidents of corruption and actions taken 10"^^xsd:string ;
    rso:sourceDocument "GRI 205_ Anti-corruption 2016.pdf"^^xsd:string ;
    schema1:identifier "Disclosure 205-3 Confirmed incidents of corruption"^^xsd:string ;
    schema1:name "Disclosure 205-3 Confirmed incidents of corruption and actions taken"^^xsd:string,
        "Disclosure 205-3 Confirmed incidents of corruption and actions taken 10"^^xsd:string ;
    schema1:text "Disclosure 205-3 Confirmed incidents of corruption and actions taken 10\\nGRI 205: Anti-corruption 2016 4\\nIntroduction\\nGRI 205: Anti-corruption 2016 contains disclosures for organizations to report information about their corruption- related impacts, and how they manage these impacts.\\nThe Standard is structured as follows:\\nSection 1 contains a requirement, which provides information about how the organization manages its corruption- related impacts. •\\nSection 2 contains three disclosures, which provide information about the organization’s corruption-related impacts. •\\nThe Glossary contains defined terms with a specific meaning when used in the GRI Standards. The terms are underlined in the text of the GRI Standards and linked to the definitions. •\\nThe Bibliography lists authoritative intergovernmental instruments and additional references used in developing this Standard. •\\nThe rest of the Introduction section provides a background on the topic, an overview of the system of GRI Standards and further information on using this Standard.\\nBackground on the topic This Standard addresses the topic of anti-corruption. In this Standard, corruption is understood to include practices such as bribery, facilitation payments, fraud, extortion, collusion, and money laundering; the offer or receipt of gifts, loans, fees, rewards, or other advantages as an inducement to do something that is dishonest, illegal, or represents a breach of trust. It can also include practices such as embezzlement, trading in influence, abuse of function, illicit enrichment, concealment, and obstructing justice.\\nCorruption is broadly linked to negative impacts, such as poverty in transition economies, damage to the environment, abuse of human rights, abuse of democracy, misallocation of investments, and undermining the rule of law. Organizations are expected by the marketplace, international norms, and stakeholders to demonstrate their adherence to integrity, governance, and responsible business practices.\\nThese concepts are covered in key instruments of the Organisation for Economic Co-operation and Development and the United Nations: see the Bibliography .\\nSystem of GRI Standards This Standard is part of the GRI Sustainability Reporting Standards (GRI Standards). The GRI Standards enable an organization to report information about its most significant impacts on the economy, environment, and people, including impacts on their human rights, and how it manages these impacts.\\nThe GRI Standards are structured as a system of interrelated standards that are organized into three series: GRI Universal Standards, GRI Sector Standards, and GRI Topic Standards (see Figure 1 in this Standard).\\nUniversal Standards: GRI 1, GRI 2 and GRI 3 GRI 1: Foundation 2021 specifies the requirements that the organization must comply with to report in accordance with the GRI Standards. The organization begins using the GRI Standards by consulting GRI 1 .\\nGRI 2: General Disclosures 2021 contains disclosures that the organization uses to provide information about its reporting practices and other organizational details, such as its activities, governance, and policies.\\nGRI 3: Material Topics 2021 provides guidance on how to determine material topics. It also contains disclosures that the organization uses to report information about its process of determining material topics, its list of material topics, and how it manages each topic.\\nSector Standards The Sector Standards provide information for organizations about their likely material topics. The organization uses the Sector Standards that apply to its sectors when determining its material topics and when determining what to report for each material topic.\\nTopic Standards The Topic Standards contain disclosures that the organization uses to report information about its impacts in relation to particular topics. The organization uses the Topic Standards according to the list of material topics it has determined using GRI 3 .\\nGRI 205: Anti-corruption 2016 5\\nFigure 1. GRI Standards: Universal, Sector and Topic Standards\\nGRI Standards\\nSector Standards Universal Standards Topic Standards\\nRequirements and principles for using the GRI Standards\\nDisclosures about the reporting organization\\nDisclosures and guidance about the organization's material topics\\nApply all three Universal Standards to your reporting\\nUse the Sector Standards that\\nSelect Topic Standards to report\\napply to your sectors\\nspecific information on your\\nmaterial topics\\nUsing this Standard This Standard can be used by any organization – regardless of size, type, sector, geographic location, or reporting experience – to report information about its corruption-related impacts.\\nAn organization reporting in accordance with the GRI Standards is required to report the following disclosures if it has determined anti-corruption to be a material topic:"^^xsd:string,
        "Disclosure 205-3 Confirmed incidents of corruption and actions taken\\nThe reporting organization shall report the following information: REQUIREMENTS\\nTotal number and nature of confirmed incidents of corruption. a.\\nTotal number of confirmed incidents in which employees were dismissed or disciplined for corruption. b.\\nTotal number of confirmed incidents when contracts with business partners were terminated or not renewed due to violations related to corruption. c.\\nPublic legal cases regarding corruption brought against the organization or its employees during the reporting period and the outcomes of such cases. d.\\nGuidance for Disclosure 205-3 For stakeholders, there is an interest in both the occurrence of incidents and an organization’s response to the incidents. Public legal cases regarding corruption can include current public investigations, prosecutions, or closed cases."^^xsd:string .

rso:Disclosure_Disclosure_206_1_Legal_actions_for_anti_competitiv a rso:Disclosure ;
    rso:belongsToTopic rso:Topic_GRI_206_Anti_competitive_Behavior_2016_3 ;
    rso:originalText "Disclosure 206-1 Legal actions for anti-competitive behavior, anti- trust, and monopoly practices\\nThe reporting organization shall report the following information: REQUIREMENTS\\na.\\nNumber of legal actions pending or completed during the reporting period regarding anti-competitive behavior and violations of anti-trust and monopoly legislation in which the organization has been identified as a participant.\\nMain outcomes of completed legal actions, including any decisions or judgements. b.\\nBackground This disclosure pertains to legal actions initiated under national or international laws designed primarily for the purpose of regulating anti-competitive behavior, anti-trust, or monopoly practices."^^xsd:string,
        "Disclosure 206-1 Legal actions for anti-competitive behavior, anti-trust, and monopoly practices 8\\nGRI 206: Anti-competitive Behavior 2016 4\\nIntroduction\\nGRI 206: Anti-competitive Behavior 2016 contains disclosures for organizations to report information about their anti- competitive behavior-related impacts, and how they manage these impacts.\\nThe Standard is structured as follows:\\nSection 1 contains a requirement, which provides information about how the organization manages its anti- competitive behavior-related impact. •\\nSection 2 contains one disclosure, which provides information about the organization’s anti-competitive behavior- related impacts. •\\nThe Glossary contains defined terms with a specific meaning when used in the GRI Standards. The terms are underlined in the text of the GRI Standards and linked to the definitions. •\\nThe Bibliography lists authoritative intergovernmental instruments used in developing this Standard. •\\nThe rest of the Introduction section provides a background on the topic, an overview of the system of GRI Standards and further information on using this Standard.\\nBackground on the topic This Standard addresses the topic of anti-competitive behavior, including anti-trust and monopoly practices.\\nAnti-competitive behavior refers to actions of an organization or its employees that can result in collusion with potential competitors, with the purpose of limiting the effects of market competition. This can include fixing prices or coordinating bids, creating market or output restrictions, imposing geographic quotas, and allocating customers, suppliers, geographic areas, or product lines.\\nAnti-trust and monopoly practices are actions of an organization that can result in collusion to erect barriers for entry to the sector, or to otherwise prevent competition. This can include unfair business practices, abuse of market position, cartels, anti-competitive mergers, and price-fixing.\\nThese concepts are covered in key instruments of the Organisation for Economic Co-operation and Development: see the Bibliography .\\nSystem of GRI Standards This Standard is part of the GRI Sustainability Reporting Standards (GRI Standards). The GRI Standards enable an organization to report information about its most significant impacts on the economy, environment, and people, including impacts on their human rights, and how it manages these impacts.\\nThe GRI Standards are structured as a system of interrelated standards that are organized into three series: GRI Universal Standards, GRI Sector Standards, and GRI Topic Standards (see Figure 1 in this Standard).\\nUniversal Standards: GRI 1, GRI 2 and GRI 3 GRI 1: Foundation 2021 specifies the requirements that the organization must comply with to report in accordance with the GRI Standards. The organization begins using the GRI Standards by consulting GRI 1 .\\nGRI 2: General Disclosures 2021 contains disclosures that the organization uses to provide information about its reporting practices and other organizational details, such as its activities, governance, and policies.\\nGRI 3: Material Topics 2021 provides guidance on how to determine material topics. It also contains disclosures that the organization uses to report information about its process of determining material topics, its list of material topics, and how it manages each topic.\\nSector Standards The Sector Standards provide information for organizations about their likely material topics. The organization uses the Sector Standards that apply to its sectors when determining its material topics and when determining what to report for each material topic.\\nTopic Standards The Topic Standards contain disclosures that the organization uses to report information about its impacts in relation to particular topics. The organization uses the Topic Standards according to the list of material topics it has determined using GRI 3 .\\nGRI 206: Anti-competitive Behavior 2016 5\\nFigure 1. GRI Standards: Universal, Sector and Topic Standards\\nGRI Standards\\nSector Standards Universal Standards Topic Standards\\nRequirements and principles for using the GRI Standards\\nDisclosures about the reporting organization\\nDisclosures and guidance about the organization's material topics\\nApply all three Universal Standards to your reporting\\nUse the Sector Standards that\\nSelect Topic Standards to report\\napply to your sectors\\nspecific information on your\\nmaterial topics\\nUsing this Standard This Standard can be used by any organization – regardless of size, type, sector, geographic location, or reporting experience – to report information about its anti-competitive behavior-related impacts.\\nAn organization reporting in accordance with the GRI Standards is required to report the following disclosures if it has determined anti-competitive behavior to be a material topic:"^^xsd:string ;
    rso:pageEnd 5,
        13 ;
    rso:pageStart 3,
        8 ;
    rso:requires rso:Requirement_2050269117745615910 ;
    rso:sectionLabel "Disclosure 206-1 Legal actions for anti-competitive behavior, anti- trust, and monopoly practices"^^xsd:string,
        "Disclosure 206-1 Legal actions for anti-competitive behavior, anti-trust, and monopoly practices 8"^^xsd:string ;
